Members of the Wales Assembly worried about “passing as puppets”
Welsh elected officials are appalled by a proposal to replace their title with MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
This proposal stems from the proposed modification of the name of the assembly, which would become the Welsh Parliament.
In the political sphere, members of the Assembly believe that this could be the subject of taunting.
A Labour elected official said his group is concerned that the acronym is “rhyming with Twp and Pwp.”
For readers not from Wales: in Welsh, twp means stupid, and pwp means crotte.
According to one of its members, Plaid Cymru (Wales Party) is not “satisfied” and proposed other formulas.
The conservative party would be “open” to a name change, but noted that between MWP and Muppet (puppet), the distance is narrow.
In this context, in Welsh, the letter w pronounces itself as the letter u in English of the Yorkshire region.
The Assembly’s Committee, which is currently preparing legislation introducing the name change, said that “the final decision concerning the denomination of the members of the Assembly belongs to them”.
The Government of Wales (Government of Wales Act of 2017) allows the Welsh Assembly the power to change its name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation on the proposal to designate the Assembly as the Welsh Parliament, which was widely supported.
On the question of the title of the members, the Commission favours Members of the Welsh Parliament (or WMP); however, a vast majority of Welsh people prefer MWP.
It seems that the members of the Assembly are making other suggestions, but the effort to reach a consensus could prove to be a real headache for Elin Jones, the Speaker of the Assembly, who is expected to submish a bill on name change in the coming weeks.
The reform legislation will include further amendments concerning the functioning of the assembly, in particular the rules relating to the exclusion of members and the design of the committee system.
The members of the Assembly will take the final decision on the issue of the name during the debates on the bill.
Macedonia : referendum on the modification of the name of the country
The Macedonians will decide on Sunday whether their country should henceforth bear the name “Republic of North Macedonia”.
The popular vote was organized in an attempt to resolve a decades-old litigation between the country and its Greek neighbor, for which Macedonia is the name of one of its provinces.
For a very long pass, Athens insists that the name of Macedonia constitutes a demand on its territory and has repeatedly imposed its veto on the accession of its neighboring country to the European Union and NATO.
The Macedonian President, Gjorge Ivanov, opposed to the referendum, indicated that he would not take into account the results.
However, supporters such as Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, maintain that the name change is only a small price to pay to join the EU and NATO.
Harlem : churches in difficulty, bells of Saint Martin reduced to silence
“The elderly people I’ve been talking to say that traditionally there’s been a bar and a church on every corner,” Adams said.
“There’s none more today.”
According to him, it is normal that the bars have become scarce.
“We don’t date in the same way anymore,” he explains.
“Before, we went to the bar like his neighbor, but that’s not the case anymore.”
As for the churches, the funds coming from the sale of buildings will run out long before what officials imagine and it worries him: “sooner or later, they will return to square one.”
He adds that churches could be replaced by condominium complexes, inhabited by individuals who will not help maintain the last shrines in the neighborhood.
“Those who buy condominiums in these buildings will largely be white people who rush the closure of all the churches, because it’s unlikely that they’ll become members,” he said.
Before Harlem became a black metropolis, white congregations built the two churches: Metropolitan Community in 1870, and Saint Martin 10 years later.
The initial White Methodist congregation abandoned the place in the 1930’s.
A black congregation, preaching around, took possession of the building.
St. Martin’s Church was taken over by a black community under the leadership of Reverend John Howard Johnson, who initiated a boycott of 125th Street shops, one of Harlem’s main shopping streets, in which blacks struggled to get employed or promoted.
In 1939, a fire ravaged the building, but Father Johnson's parishioners were preparing to rebuild and ordered a new chime,
that the son and successor of Father Johnson in the leadership of the church of St. Martin, the Reverend David Johnson, proudly called the “bells of the poor.”
Tiffany Ng (University of Michigan), the professional who played it in July, gave him a completely different name: “a cultural treasure” and “an irreplacable historical instrument.”
She also mentions that it is the world’s first carillon played by a black musician, Dionisio A. Lind, who, 18 years ago, took place in front of Riverside Church’s larger chime.
Merriweather states that Saint Martin did not replace him.
In recent months there has been a lot of talk about the shaking of architects and contractors, provoked by the secular elite of the church on the one hand, and the episcopal diocese on the other.
In July, the parish council, a governing body composed of lay officials, informed the diocese that he was concerned that the latter wanted to impose the costs incurred, even though he did not participate in the recruitment of professionals.
Some parishioners deplored the lack of transparency from the diocese.
California: A 13-year-old boy injured by a shark during a lobster fishing game
According to authorities, a shark attacked and injured a 13-year-old last Saturday while fishing for lobster in California on the opening day of the season.
The attack occurred just before 7 a.m. near Beacon Beach in Encinitas.
Chad Hammel told San Diego’s TV channel KSWB-TV that he had been diving with friends for half an hour on Saturday morning when he heard the boy scream for help and begged paddling to him to get him out of the water.
Initially, Chad thought that the young boy was shouting with joy to have had a catch, and then he understood that in reality he
had been bitten.
Arriving near the young man, Hammel noticed that he had an open fracture of the collarbone.
“I was screaming for everyone to come out of the water: There’s a shark,” Chad says.
The boy was evacuated to Rady Children Hospital in San Diego, where he is still in critical condition.
The species of the shark causing the attack is not known.
At a press conference, rescue chief Larry Giles informed that a shark had been spotted a few weeks earlier, but it wasn’t a dangerous species.
He added that the victim suffered traumatic injuries on the upper part of the torso.
The authorities closed access from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami in Ecinitas for 48 hours to conduct an investigation as well as for security reasons.
Giles indicated that there are more than 135 species of sharks, but most are considered non-hazardous.
Offensive plans for Sainsbury’s in the British Aesthetics Market
Sainsbury’s handmade on Boots, Superdrug and Debenhams and fills them with shelves similar to those of supermarkets, filled with aesthetic and cosmetic products and managed by specialists in the field.
As part of a major breakthrough in Britain’s 2.8 billion-pound sterling aesthetic market, which continues to grow as sales of fashion and home items are falling, rays of more extensive beauty products will be piloted in 11 stores and applied to others if the experience proves conclusive.
Supermarkets are now investing in the beauty market with the aim of reusing the space previously occupied by televisions, microwaves and other household items.
Sainsbury’s indicated that it would increase the volume of its offerings up to 3 000 cosmetic items, which will include for the first time brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw.
L’Oreal’s, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees existing ranges will also have more space in areas similar to shops such as Boots.
The supermarket is also relaunching its Boutique make-up line to offer a majority of products not tested on animals, an ever-increasing criterion among young consumers.
In addition, perfume seller Fragrance Shop will test dealerships in two stores in Sainsbury’s, the first opened last week in Croydon, South London, and a second scheduled to open later this year in Selly Oak (Birmingham).
Supermarket executives need to dig their heads to attract consumers, who are increasingly tending to shop online and heading to convenience stores for everyday food products.
Mike Coupe, chief executive of Sainsbury’s, said outlets will increasingly resemble supermarkets, as the supermarket chain is trying to counter discount Aldi and Lidl stores by offering more services and non-food products.
Sainsbury’s has set up Argos outlets in hundreds of stores and has also opened the doors of several Habitat stores, since the purchase of the two chains 2 years ago, which, according to the group, made it possible to propel sales of everyday consumer goods and make acquisitions profitable.
Previous attempts to restructure the shelves of pharmaceutical and cosmetic items of the British chain had failed.
In the early 2000’s, Sainsbury’s attempted a partnership with Boots, but ended following a controversy regarding the distribution of profits from pharmacies located in supermarkets.
It is after selling 3 years ago its 281 pharmacies for an amount of 125 million pounds to Celesio, the owner of the pharmaceutical chain Lloyds, that the new strategy was implemented.
Lloyds had to play a role of introducing a wide range of high-end skin care brands, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy in 4 stores.
Paul Mills-Hicks, Commercial Director of Sainsbury’s, announced that the appearance of beauty product shelves has been modified to improve customer management.
“We have also invested in training so that our staff can offer sound advice.
Our range of brands is designed to meet any type of need and, thanks to the beautification of our shelves and the ideal situation of our establishments, our dominance of the cosmetics market is now unquestionable and modifies purchasing habits,” added Mr. Mills-Hicks.
Anger by Peter Jones after Holly Willoughby withdrew £11 million
Wrath of the star of the television program Dragon’s Den, Peter Jones, after the announcement of the withdrawal of host Holly Willoughby from a £11 million contract related to his home decoration brand, to devote himself to his new contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV
Willoughby doesn’t have time to take care of Truly’s interior decoration and accessories brand.
The activity of the two protagonists has been compared to Gwyneth Paltrow’s Goop brand.
The 37-year-old This Morning host announced on Instagram that she was pulling out.
Holly Willoughby mannied Peter Jones, the star of Dragon’s Den, withdrew from their fruitful textile and interior design business at the last minute to focus on his new exclusive contracts with Marks & Spencer and ITV.
Sources said Jones was furious when the darling TV child revealed, at a meeting under tension at Dragon’s Den Star’s commercial empire headquarters in Marlow (Buckinghamshire), that his new contracts, up to 1.5 million pounds sterling, no longer allowed him to take care of Truly, their decorating brand and interior accessories.
The Truly brand has been equated with that of actress Gwyneth Paltrow, Goop, and could have doubled the value of the facilitator’s fortune estimated at £11 million.
When 30-year-old Willoughby announced on Instagram that she was leaving Truly, Jones jumped into her private jet to join one of her second homes outside Britain.
According to a source, “Truly was by far one of Holly’s priorities
and in the process of securing its future for the next 2 decades.
Her decision left everyone speechless.
Nobody could imagine what was going to happen on Tuesday, the launch was so close.
Marlow’s headquarters are full of products ready for sale.”
Specialists believe that the departure of the host of This Morning, which is one of Britain’s most “bankable” celebrities, could make the company lose millions, due to significant investments in products ranging from cushions or candles, to clothing or indoors, and the risk of a delayed launch.
Moreover, it could end a lengthy friendship.
Willoughby and her husband Dan Baldwin, parents of 3 children, had been close friends of Jones and his wife Tara Capp for 10 years.
Holly Willoughby created Truly with Capp in 2016; 52-year-old Jones joined the group as president in March of the same year.
The couples were going on vacation together and Peter Jones owns 40% of the shares in Baldwin’s television production company.
Willoughby is expected to become brand ambassador for M&S and will take the role of Ant McPartlin as host of ITV’s I’m A Celebrity program.
A source close to Jones said last night he did not want to comment on his business activities.
The debate was stormy, but in the end we love each other
Trump mocked the media criticizing him because of his comment that some consider “unworthily of a president,” and to be so positive about the North Korean leader.
“Why did President Trump give up so much?”
He laughed, taking a television journalist voice.
“I didn’t give up anything at all.”
He said Kim Jong-un was in favour of holding a second meeting, after Trump described the first, which took place in Singapore in June, as a major step towards denuclearizing North Korea.
Negotiations on this denuclearization, however, have stalled.
More than three months after the June summit in Singapore, North Korea’s Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho told world leaders at the UN General Assembly on Saturday that there is no U.S. “conclusive response” to North Korea’s first disarmament measures.
On the contrary, he deplored that the United States continued to impose sanctions to maintain pressure.
In his rally speech Trump offers a far more optimistic perspective.
“It’s all right with North Korea,” he said.
We're close to armed conflict.
“Millions would have been killed.
Now we have an excellent relationship.”
According to Trump, his efforts to improve relations with Kim resulted in positive results: an end to missile testing, the release of hostages and the remains of North American soldiers returned to their families.
He also justified his unusual approach to dealings with Kim.
“It’s so easy to play the role of president, but instead of having 10 000 people trying to access this crack-like arena, there’s barely more than 200 standing in front of me,” he criticized, pointing to the crowd.
Tsunami: Devastated island and hundreds killed in Indonesia
Following the Lombok earthquake, help from foreign NGOs was supposedly not necessary.
More than 10% of Lombok’s population was scattered, but no state of national disaster has been decreed as a requirement for the onset of international aid.
“Unfortunately, they’ve repeatedly made it clear that they don’t want international aid, which makes things a little more complicated,” Sumbung said.
As Save the Childen sets up a team to travel to Palu, she still doesn’t know if fieldwork will be allowed.
Sutopo, spokesman for the National Disaster Management Agency, said the Indonesian authorities were assessing the situation in Palu to determine whether international agencies would be allowed to contribute to humanitarian aid.
In spite of the tremors it is constantly experiencing, the country is sorely lacking the means to protect itself from the whims of nature.
Shelters have been built in Aceh, but they are scarce on other coastal areas.
Although a warning has been communicated, there has been no siren of alarm in Palu, which has no doubt contributed to human losses.
Even in ideal conditions, moving from one Indonesian island to another is not easy.
Natural disasters further complicate the use of transportation.
A hospital ship that had been stationed in Lombok to treat the victims, is now on its way to Palu, but it will take at least 3 days to reach the scene of the new disaster.
Indonesia’s President Joko Widodo has placed dilapidated infrastructure reform at the center of his election campaign and has spent considerable sums of money to restore roads and railways.
The lack of funds, however, undermined Joko’s government, called to stand before voters next year.
The Indonesian head of state is also under pressure from the persistence of inter-ethnic tensions in the country, where members of the Islamic majority practice their faith in a more conservative way.
More than a thousand Indonesians lost their lives and tens of thousands have been kicked out of their homes, amid clashes in the streets between Christians and Muslims, armed with machetes, bows and arrows and other rudimentary weapons.
Watch: Liverpool forward Daniel Sturridge achieves equalisation against Chelsea
Daniel Sturridge saves a loss to Liverpool in the Premier League against Chelsea, scoring in the 89th minute on Saturday at Stamford Bridge (London).
Sturridge received a pass from Xherdan Shaqiri while he was about 30 metres away from Chelsea’s goals, and his team was led from 1 to 0.
He shot in the ball on his left, before escaping a shot towards the second post.
The balloon flew over the repair surface before heading to the upper right corner of the net.
The ball eventually reached Kepa Arrizabalaga and ended its path at the bottom of the cage.
“I was just trying to place myself, to grab the ball, but players like Shaq are always pulling as far as possible, so I’ve tried to save as much time as possible,” Sturridge told LiverpoolFC.com.
“I saw that Kante was approaching to control and without really much thinking I decided to shoot.”
Chelsea led 1-0 at half-time after a goal from Belgian star Eden Hazard in the 25th minute.
The Blues forward tracked a pass towards Mateo Kovacic, before heading to the midfield and sprinting Liverpool side.
Kovacic made a quick back-and-forth in midfield,
then made a magnificent shot in depth that led Hazard into the surface.
Hazard surpassed the defense and finished his race in the net of the second post, with a shot from the left foot behind Liverpool goalkeeper Alisson Becker.
Liverpool will face Neapolitans in the Champions League group stage, Wednesday at 3 p.m. at the San Paolo stadium in Naples, Italy.
Chelsea will face Videoton in UEFA’s Europa League, at 3 p.m. this Thursday in London.
Indonesia: The number of victims of the tsunami rises to 832
The disaster management agency said Sunday morning that the toll of the earthquake and tsunami reports 832 deaths.
Many residents are stranded under the rubble of buildings destroyed by the 7.5-magnitude earthquake that shaked the country on Friday and triggered 6-metre-high waves, Sutopo spokesman Purwo Nugroho told a news conference.
With more than 380 000 inhabitants, the city of Palu was dotted with debris from the collapsed buildings.
Polices arrest 32-year-old man suspected of stabbing a woman
A murder investigation was opened after a woman’s corpse was found in Birkenhead (Merseyside) this morning.
At 7:55 a.m., the 44-year-old stabbing woman was uncovered on Grayson Mews John Street, and a 32-year-old man suspected of murder was arrested.
Polices are urging the residents of the neighborhood who allegedly saw or heard something to give their testimony.
Police Captain Brian O’Hagan said: “The investigation has just begun, but I would like to ask anyone around John Street in Birkenhead who would have seen or heard anything suspicious of contacting us.
In particular, I appeal to taxi drivers, who could have captured something on their dashcam and begged them to provide us with any crucial information for our investigation.”
A police spokeswoman confirmed that the murdered woman was found at a Birkenhead property in which she resided.
This morning, people claiming to know the woman came to the scene of the crime and asked questions about where she was discovered.
The investigation is ongoing and the police have indicated that they are informing the close relatives of the victim.
A taxi driver has just been denied access to his apartment in Grayson Mews, the police not allowing anyone to enter or leave the building.
The news left him speechless.
Residents have just been informed that the building would be inaccessible for several hours.
A police officer said the entire area was considered a crime scene.
A woman in tears appeared at the scene.
Repeating, “It’s too horrible.”
At 2 p.m., two police vans were stationed inside the cord, and one outside.
Several officers were guarding the building on the inner side of the cord.
Anyone with information is asked to send a message to @MerPolCC, to call 101 or to anonymously contact the organization Echec au crime at 0800 555 111, indicates the register 247 of September 30.
Parliament : the statue of Cromwell, last victim of an attempt to rewrite history
Its prohibition would be the just return of things, in view of the acts of destruction, countless religious and cultural possessions of England, committed by its Puritan supporters, such as fanatical Taliban.
However, the Cromwell Society found Mr. Crick’s proposal “unreasonable” and called it a “revisionist attempt.”
The president of the Cromwell Society, John Goldsmith, says that, “Given the current debate around the removal of statues, it was clear that Oliver Cromwell’s erected outside the Palace of Westminster would not be spared.
Cromwell did not ordain or conduct iconoclasm in the English civil wars.
It is likely that his person was sacrificed for the actions committed by his ancestor Thomas in the previous century.
The superb representation of Cromwell, sculpted by Sir William Hamo Thorneycroft, attests to the consideration we had for him in the 19th century and belongs to the historiography of a personality that many of us still celebrate.”
Mr Goldsmitm said: “Cromwell is considered by many, perhaps more at the end of the 19th century than today, as a defender of parliament against external pressure, I mean the monarchony of course.
Whether this representation is accurate or not is a continuous historical debate.
What is certain is that the conflict broke out in the middle of the 17th century shaped the further development of our nation, and Cromwell is a particularly recognizable figure who represents a side of the divide.
His accomplishments as a Lord Protector also deserve to be emphasized and commemorated.”
China : a pig kills a breeder
According to local media, a farmer was attacked and killed by a pig in a market in southeastern China.
Identified as M, Yuan, the man has been found dead, the artery cut and covered in blood near a pigsty on the market in Liupanshui in Guizhou province, the South China Morning Post said Sunday.
A pork breeder prepares to inject vaccines to pigs in a pigsty on May 30, 2005 in Xining, Qinghai Province, China.
He is said to have traveled accompanied by his cousin from the neighboring province of Yunnan on Wednesday, in order to sell 15 pigs on the market.
The next morning his cousin found him dead and discovered that a door of the nearby pigsty was open.
He claims that a huge male pig was inside it and that blood was flowing from his mouth.
A forensic report confirms that the animal weighing 250 kg mutilated the breeder to death.
According to Guiyang Evening News, Mr. Wu, the breeder’s cousin, said that “his legs were crushed and covered with blood.”
Security cameras show Mr. Yuan coming to the market at 4:40 a.m. on Thursday to feed his animals.
Her body was discovered about an hour later.
The pig behind Mr. Yuan’s death did not belong to the breeder or his cousin.
A market official told the Evening News that the animal was locked up to avoid any other incidents and allow police officers to gather evidence.
The family of breeder Mr. Yuan and market leaders are reportedly negotiating compensation.
Although the facts are rare, pig attacks against people have already been reported.
In 2016 at a farm in Massachusetts, a pig assaulted a woman and her husband, seriously injuring him.
Ten years earlier, a 295-kilogram pig caught a Welsh breeder on his tractor before his wife managed to keep him away.
In 2012, when an Oregon breeder had just been devoured by his pigs, a Manitoba farmer told CBC News that it is not an aggressive animal in principle, but that the taste of blood can cause this type of behavior.
“They’re pretty player animals,
Very curious kids... they have no intention of hurting.
You just have to show them the respect they deserve,” he said.
The remnants of Hurricane Rosa are expected to cause heavy rainfall in the southeastern United States
In line with forecasts, Hurricane Rosa weakens as it moves over the colder waters of Mexico’s northeast coast.
However, Rosa will cause showers in northeastern Mexico and southwestern United States over the coming days.
Winds blew at 135 km/h (grade 1 hurricane) from 5 a.m. Eastern Time Sunday, while Rosa was 620 km southwest of Punta Eugenia (Mexico).
The hurricane is expected to move north on Sunday.
During this time, a depression begins to form over the Pacific Ocean and circulates to the east, towards the west coast of the United States. As it gets closer to the Baja California Peninsula in the form of a storm, Hurricane Rosa will result in an intense tropical humidity to the north, in the southwestern United States.
It will fall up to 25 cm of rain in parts of Mexico on Monday.
Then tropical humidity combined with the approaching depression will create widespread torrential rains in the southwest over the coming days.
At the local level, precipitation reaching between 2.5 and 10 cm will cause dangerous sudden floods, debris flows and potential landslides in the desert.
Showers will reach a rate of 5 to 7.5 cm per hour in places, especially in southern Nevada and Arizona, due to the intensity of tropical humidity.
It should fall between 5 and 10 cm of rain in areas of the south-west, including much of Arizona.
Disseminated tropical rains are likely to cause sudden flooding and rapid deterioration of conditions.
It is strongly discouraged to venture into the desert on foot due to the threat of tropical rainfall.
Intense rains could convert canyons into unleashed rivers and thunderstorms will produce gusts of wind and whirlwinds of dust in places.
The approaching depression will result in abundant local rains in some southern parts of the country, on the California coastline.
The rainfall totals can reach more than 1.3 cm, which could result in low flows of debrish and make the slippery.
It could be the first rains of the dry season in the region.
Dispersed tropical showers will hit Arizona on Sunday at the end of the day and early Monday morning, and then intensify later Monday and Tuesday.
Heavy rainfall will occur across the country on Tuesday and will last until Wednesday.
Significant variations in temperature are expected for October across the United States due to the cooling of the Arctic, but the tropic climate will remain warm.
this sometimes causes a radical change of time over short distances.
The central region of the United States, which will suffer considerable temperature differences on Sunday, is a perfect example.
Between Kansas City, Missouri and Omaha, Nebraska, or between Saint Louis and Des Moines (Iowa), the difference is almost 20 degrees.
Over the next few days, persistent summer heat will attempt to infiltrate and intensify again.
Most of the central and eastern U.S. is projected to experience a warm start in October, with temperatures running around 26 degrees covering the southern plains and other areas of the north-east.
Mercury could reach 26 degrees in New York on Tuesday, about 10 degrees above the season average.
Several-day forecasts indicate that temperatures are expected to be above-average in the eastern U.S. in the first fortnight of October.
Brett Kavanaugh’s trial attracts more than 20 million viewers
Broadcasted on 6 television channels on Thursday, Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanauaugh and Christine Blasey Ford, the woman accusing her of sexually assaulting her in the 1980s, were followed by more than 20 million people.
As the political impasse persists, broadcasting services have halted their usual Friday program to announce a theater: a deal imagined by Jeff Flake, the Arizona Senator, to allow the FBI to conduct an investigation into the charges for a week.
In front of the Senate Judiciary’s Committee, Christine Ford assured 100% that Kavanaugh, under the effect of alcohol, had engaged in touching her person and had tried to remove her clothes during an evening among high school students.
Kavanaugh, in a passionate tirade, claimed to be 100% certain that this had never happened.
According to a report by Nielsen, the trial likely reached an audience of over 20.4 million people.
The company calculated the number of viewers from CBS, ABC, NBC, CNN, Fox News Channel and MSNBC.
Figures from other channels broadcasting the trial, including PBS, C-SPAN and Fox Business Network, weren’t immediately available.
Moreover, Nielsen does not always manage to estimate the number of people watching TV in the office.
By way of comparison, the trial attracted as many people as a football game or the Oscars.
According to Nielsen, Fox News Channel, whose hosts largely backed Kavanaugh’s nomination, was the most-watched channel on Audition Day, with an average of 5.69 million viewers.
ABC ranked second with an audience up to 3.26 million.
CBS (3.1 million), NBC (2.94 million), MSNBC (2.89 million) and CNN (2.52 million), according to Nielsen.
Interest didn’t weaken once the hearing was over.
Flake was at the center of Friday’s drama.
Following the moderate Republican’s office issued a statement that he would vote for Kavanaugh, CNN and CBS cameras unveiled a scene in which the senator is caught by protesters as he was trying to pick up the elevator to get to the panel hearing.
Flake lowered his eyes for several minutes, as CNN retransmitted the sequence of live arrest.
“Don’t look elsewhere, look at me,” one of the women said.
“Do you really think he’s telling the truth?
“You have power when so many women are destitute.”
Before the elevator’s doors closed, Flake indicated that his office issued a statement and that he would say more at the committee meeting.
All cable and broadcast network channels covered the session live a few hours later, as the Judicial Committee prepared to vote the procedure to advance Kavanaugh’s appointment by the entire Senate.
Flake indicated, however, that a week-long investigation should first be conducted by the FBI on the accusations made against the candidate, which the minority Democrats demanded.
Flake let himself be convinced in part by chatting with his friend, Democratic Senator Chris Coons.
He made his decision after meeting with Coons and several other senators afterwards.
His choice weighed heavily in the balance, because the Republicans would indeed not have obtained the votes to approve Kavanaugh’s appointment without a prior investigation.
President Trump allowed the FBI to investigate allegations of sexual assault by Brett Kavanaugh.
Britain: May accuses opponents of Brexit of doing “small politics”
In an interview with Sunday Times, Prime Minister Theresa May accused critics of his plans to leave the European Union, to make “small politics” to the detriment of Britain’s future and national interest.
May arrived at the Conservative Party Conference in Birmingham on September 29, 2018.
In another exchange with the newspaper, which published a photo of Theresa May on the cover, its former foreign minister, Boris Johnson continued his attacks on his so-called Chequers plans, claiming that Britain’s free trade proposal and the European Union was “neither ridiculous.”
Wayde Sims: Police arrest Dyteon Simpson, suspected of being responsible for LSU player’s death
Police have arrested a fatal shooting suspect who resulted in the death of Wayde Sims, a 20-year-old LSU basketball player.
Dyteon Simpson, also 20, has been jailed and charged with second-degree murder, Baton Rouge Police Department said.
Authorities released a video of the clash between Sims and Simpson, and police said Sims losted his glasses during the altercation.
Found at the scene of the crime, the glasses carried the DNA of Simpson, according to WAFB, a subsidiary of the CBS group.
During his interrogation, Simpson confessed to the police that he was responsible for Wayde's death.
According to his lawyer, his bail has been set at US$350,000.
The office of the parish of Baton-Red published a preliminary report Friday, which determines that the death was caused by an injury by a bullet that passed through the head and lodged in the neck.
The police department acknowledges the assistance received by the Fugitive Task Force of Louisiana State, the forensic laboratory of the State Police, the police of Southern University, as well as citizens, in the investigation that led to the arrest.
LSU sporting director Joe Alleva thanked law enforcement for their “commitment and quest for justice.”
Sims was only 20 years old.
1.98 m for 98 kg, the player grew up in Baton-Rouge, where his father, Wayne, also played LSU basketball.
On average, it scored 5.6 points and made 2.6 rebounds per game in the last season.
On Friday morning, Will Wade, LSU’s basketball coach, said the team was “devastated and in shock” after News of Wayde’s death.
“We dread this type of event all the time,” Wade said.
Mexico : a volcano spits ashes
Ash escaping from Popocatepetl Volcano has reached the southern districts of Mexico’s capital.
On Saturday, the National Center for Disaster Reduction recommended that Mexicans move away from the volcano, after recording a peak of activity in the crater, with 183 gas and ash emissions in 24 hours.
The center monitored several rumblings and tremors from Popocatepetl.
On social networks, the images showed thin layers of ashes covering car windshields in neighborhoods of Mexico City, such as Xochimilco.
Geophysicists have observed an increase in activity at the level of the volcano located 72 km from the capital, since an earthquake with a magnitude of 7.1 shaken central Mexico in September 2017.
The volcano, nicknamed Don Goyo, has been active since 1994.
Catalonia: clashes between the police and separatists in the run-up to the anniversary of the independence vote
On Saturday, six arrests took place in Barcelona after clashes between protestering protesters and riot polices, as thousands of opponents united with protests to mark the first anniversary of Catalonia’s divided referendum.
Before being repressed by riot police, masked separatists threw eggs and colorful powder at them, creating clouds of dust in the streets, which usually don’t depopulate tourists.
Scuffles also broke out later in the day, while the police used batons to contain the clashes.
For several hours, independence groups chanting “neither oblivion nor forgiveness” faced unionist supporters shouting “long life in Spain”.
14 protesters with minor injuries were cured, according to the local press.
A year after the referendum of October 1st, judged illegal by Madrid but celebrated by Catalans in favor of separation, tensions still remain high in the independence region.
An overwhelming majority of voters chose independence, despite a low turnout rate, with opponents of secession having largely boycotted the referendum.
According to the Catalan authorities, nearly 1 000 people were injured last year after police tried to prevent the Catalans from accessing polling stations in the region.
Separatist groups camped on Friday night to block a demonstration in support of the national police.
It still took place, but was forced to take a different route.
Narcis Termes, a 68-year-old electrician who participated with his wife in the separatist demonstration, said he no longer had any hope for Catalonia’s independence.
“Last year we witnessed a great moment.
“My parents were crying for joy at being able to vote, but now we’re at an impasse,” he said.
In spite of their capital victory, although slim, in the regional elections last December, the Catalan independence parties have struggled to maintain the momentum this year, with many popular leaders being exiled or detained while awaiting trial for holding the referendum and declaring Catalonia’s independence.
Joan Puig, a 42-year-old mechanist who films the demonstration in support of the police on her phone, says politicians are responsible for blocking the situation, on both sides.
“The tension is only rising,” he regrets.
Oriol Junqueras, one of the 9 Catalan leaders in prison awaiting a judgment since last year, announced his intention to present his candidacy in the European Parliament’s elections next year.
“This is the best way for me to denounce the regression of democratic values and the repression unleashed by the Spanish government,” he added.
Arrestation in Londonderry: Men hit a house in the car
Three men between the ages of 33, 34 and 39, were arrested aftering a house in Londonderry on several occasions.
The incident took place in Ballynagard Crescent on Thursday around 7:30 p.m. local time.
Police Captain Bob Blemmings said the gates and walls were damaged.
The car would also have been shot by arrow.
Livingston wins match 1-0 against Rangers thanks to Menga
Dolly Menga's first goal ensures Livingston's victory
Recently in rank, Livingston’s team surprised the Rangers by imposing a second victory in 18 games to Steven Gerrard, the current coach of Ibrox.
Menga’s goal made all the difference and allowed Gary Holt’s team to place themselves in 2nd in the standings, tied with Hibernian.
Gerrard’s men are without an away victory in the Premiership league this season, and are expected to face the Hearts team, which leads by 8 points on Sunday.
But before, the Rangers will meet the Vienna Rapid for the Europa League this Thursday.
As for the Livingston team, it extends its undefeated course in the division by 6 games, and its head coach Holt, who has replaced Kenny Miller since last month, may boast of not having lost the slightest encounter yet.
Livingston has missed an opportunity against blunt opponents
Holt’s team could have taken the lead long before scoring, as their direct approach put the Rangers in trouble.
Scott Robinson tried to break through but remained stuck in front of the opposing goal; followed a late intervention by Alan Lithgow, on a head pass from Craig Halkett.
The Livingston team let the Rangers own the ball, knowing they could embarrass them through tactics worked.
That’s where the decisive goal came in.
The Rangers conceded a free-kick and the Livingston attempted an opening; Declan Gallagher and Robinson allied themselves to corner Menga, who took a touch and scored a midfield goal goal.
At this point, the Rangers dominated the game but found themselves facing impenetrable defense and goaltender Liam Kelly was rarely worried.
The second halve experienced the same pace, although Alfredo Morelos forced Kelly’s arrest.
Rangers goaltender Allan McGregor stopped Scott Pittman’s strike, while Lithgow managed to thwart one of Livingston’s plans.
The Livingston’s goalkeeper pushed back all attempts and protected his cage, while two penalties were denied by the referee, one for Halkett’s provocation against Glenn Middleton and the other for a foil.
The "Phenomenon" of Livingston - Analysis
Alasdair Lamont of BBC Scotland to Tony Macaroni Arena
A stunning performance and results for Livingston.
They have all been excellent and continue to exceed expectations on this ascending slope.
Their style and composition of the team have hardly changed since their return to the top, but the credit goes largely to Holt for having since his arrival motivated a team
It has many great players.
Captain Halkett’s defense was grandiose and beautifully organized, and Menga kept Connor Goldson and Joe Worrall in suspense for the duration of the game.
Unfortunately, the Rangers were not inspired.
Despite a good level with Gerrard, they did not live up to their past talent.
They missed one last opportunity and managed to break through the opposing side only once; this is a warning for the Rangers, who find themselves in the middle of the painting.
President Erdogan’s mixed reception in Cologne
On Saturday 29 September, the Turkish and German leaders met around a breakfast in Berlin in a relaxed atmosphere.
This is the last day of President Erdogan’s controversial visit to Germany, which aimed to improve relations between NATO allies.
Various issues have been addressed, such as human rights, freedom of the press and Turkey’s accession to the EU.
Erdogan then flew to Cologne in preparation for the inauguration of one of the largest mosques.
One of the most important Turkish communities abroad lives in this city.
Invoking security reasons, the police prevented 25 000 protesters from gathering outside the building, while many supporters were able to approach to perceive their president.
Hundreds of anti-Erdogan protesters, mostly Kurdish, made their voices heard, condemning both Erdogan’s policies and the German government’s decision to welcome him to his country.
The cleavage that emerges from the protests reflects Erdogan’s dual vision: a visitor greeted as a hero by some Germans of Turkish descent and described as an autocrate by others.
Deptford road accident: a cyclist dies following a collision with a car
In London, a cyclist died in a collision involving a car.
The accident occurred around 10h15 local time, near the junction between Bestwood Street and Evelyn Street, a busy road from Deptford to the southeast of the city.
The driver of the vehicle stopped, rescue arrived at the scene, but the man died at the scene of the accident.
This accident occurred several months after the death of a cyclist on Chiders Street, about 1.5 km from the accident that took place on Saturday.
The London police said the officers were doing everything possible to identify the individual and warn his relatives.
Roads have been closed and bus deviations have been set up. Motorists are advised to avoid the area.
Long Lartin prison: 6 officers injured during a riot
The head of high-security men’s prison said six prison officers were injured in a riot.
The riot began at HMP Long Lartin prison in Worcestershire around 09h30 local time on Sunday. It is still not under control.
Specialized anti-riot agents were sent to unblock the situation, involving 8 inmates and contained in a wing of the prison.
At the scene, the officers were cured for minor injuries in the face.
A spokeswoman for the prison management said: “Specially trained personnel have been deployed to restore order in HMP Long Lartin prison.
Six officers were treated for injuries.
We don’t tolerate violence in our prisons. Officials will have to respond to the police authorities and will be able to have their sentences extended.”
More than 500 prisoners, including criminals considered the most dangerous in the country, are incarcerated in HM Long Lartin prison.
In June, the director of the prison was hospitalized following an assault by a prisoner.
In October last year, anti-riot agents were sent to the prison to stop a serious disturbance: prisoners had attacked the staff with billiard balls.
Hurricane Rosa: Phoenix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City threatened by flash floods (which could be beneficial for drought-affected areas)
It’s rare for a tropical depression to hit Arizona, but that’s what’s probably going to happen early next week. Hurricane Rosa is heading to the south-west desert with what’s left of energy. This region could be affected by sudden floods.
The National Meteorological Service has already issued flood alerts for Monday and Tuesday for western Arizona, southern and eastern Nevada, southeastern California and Utah, including the cities of Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Rosa is expected to head directly to Phoenix on Tuesday, with heralding rains as early as Monday night.
The Phoenix-Based National Meteorological Service said in a tweet that “only ten tropical cyclones have retained the stage of depression or tropical storm within a 300-kilometer perimeter around Phoenix since 1950!
Hurricane Katrina (1967) stopped 64 km from the Arizona border.”
The National Hurricane Center’s latest estimates forecast 5 to 10 cm of precipitation, and up to 15 cm in places in Mogollon Rim, Arizona.
Other areas of the south-western desert including the central area of the Rockies and the Great Bassin are expected to record 2.5 to 5 cm of precipitation, with total isolated measurements of up to 10 cm.
Rosa rains could be life-saving for drought-affected areas that aren’t exposed to the risk of sudden flooding.
Even if the risk of flooding should not be taken lightly, the rain could be beneficial for the south-west region, given the current drought situation.
According to the U.S. drought monitoring system, just over 40% of Arizona is experiencing at least an extreme-category drought, the second most serious category,” weather.com reports.
Hurricane Rosa begins its journey on the coast in the Lower California Peninsula.
Still of great power on Sunday morning and generating winds of up to 135 km/h, Rosa has been located 620 km from Punta Eugenia (Mexico) and travels to the north at a speed of 20 km/h.
The storm meets the cold waters of the Pacific and therefore loses its strength.
It is expected to reach the Mexican coast with the strength of a hurricane Monday afternoon or Monday night.
In some parts of Mexico, precipitation could be massive and could lead to a risk of flooding.
“In total, between 7.5 and 15 cm of precipitation is expected from Lower California to the north-west of Sonora and can reach up to 25 cm,” weather.com said.
Rosa will then cross Mexico to the north, with the force of a tropical storm, and reach the border of Arizona early in the early morning as a tropical depression. It will cross Arizona and southern Utah in the evening of Tuesday.
“Torrential rains are expected in Lower California, north-west of Sonora and in the south-western desert and are the main danger,” the National Hurricane Center said.
It is estimated that this precipitation will result in life-threatening sudden floods and debris flows in arid areas, as well as landslides in mountainous areas.
Midsomer Norton Attack: Four Arrests for Attempted Murder
Three teenagers and a 20-year-old man have been arrested on charges of attempted homicide after a 16-year-old boy was stabbed in Somerset.
The teenager was found in Midsomer Norton’s Excelsior Terrace on Saturday around 4am local time.
He has been sent to the hospital. His condition is currently stable.
Avon and Sommerset police said they had arrested the four young people (aged 17, 18 and 20) in the night on Radstoct’s side.
Officers appealed to people who may have filmed the scene with their phones.
Trump says Kavanaugh has fallen victim to the wickedness and anger of Democrats
“A vote for Judge Kavanaugh is a vote against the ruthless and outrageous methods of the Democratic Party,” Trump said at a rally in Wheeling, West Virginia.
Trump claimed that Kavanaugh had fallen victim to the wickedness and anger of the Democrats throughout his nominating process.
Kavanaugh spoke to Congress on Thursday, emotionally rejecting Christine Blasey Ford’s allegations, who accused her of sexually assaulting her several decades ago when they were teenagers.
Ford also testified at the hearing.
The president said Saturday that “the American people have witnessed Kavanaugh’s intelligence and courage” today.
“A vote in the sense of Judge Kavanaugh is a vote in the direction of one of the most accomplished jurists, with a brilliant career in public service,” he told the crowd of supporters gathered in West Virginia.
The president referred indirectly to Kavanaugh’s appointment when he spoke about the importance of Republican participation in midterm elections.
“We are five weeks away from one of the most important elections in our lives.
“I’m not a candidate, but I’m more involved than ever,” he said.
“That’s why I’m fighting on every front for great candidates.”
Trump said the Democrats were on a “resistance and obstruction” mission.
The Senate’s first decisive procedural vote for Kavanaugh’s appointment is expected to take place by Friday, according to a statement from a Republican Party collaborator at CNN.
Indonesia: An earthquake and a tsunami claim hundreds of lives, the toll continues to rise
On Saturday, authorities said at least 384 people had perished, most of them washed away by huge waves that fell on the beaches while an earthquake, followed by a tsunami, struck the Indonesian island of Celebes.
On Friday, hundreds of people gathered for a festival on the beach in Palu, when six-meter-high waves overwhelmed the beam at nightfall. They took many lives and destroyed everything in their path.
The 7.5-magnitude quarry preceded the tsunami.
“When the threat of the tsunami arose yesterday, everyone was still on the beach. People didn’t flee immediately, it cost them their lives,” Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, spokesman for the National Disaster Management Agency (BNPB), told a news conference in Jakarta.
“The tsunami didn’t happen alone. It dragged cars, pieces of wood, houses, it took everything with it,” Nugroho said, adding that the tsunami had shifted into the open sea at a speed of 800 km/h before hitting the coast.
Some people were able to save their lives by climbing trees, he adds.
Nearly 16,700 people have been evacuated to 24 centres in Palu.
Aerial images published by the Disaster Management Agency show destroyed buildings and shops, deformed and collapsed bridges, as well as a mosque surrounded by water.
The aftershocks continued to shake the coastal city on Saturday.
Tremors were felt by 2.4 million people.
The Indonesian Agency for the Evaluation and Application of Technologies (BPPT) said the energy released by Friday’s earthquake was 200 times greater than the power of the atomic bomb released on Hiroshima during World War II.
The geographical location of the city, which borders a long narrow bay, could have amplified the size of the tsunami, she explained.
According to Nugroho, the damage is considerable; thousands of homes, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels have collapsed.
Bodies were found trapped under the rubble of ruined buildings, he added, adding that 540 people were injured and 29 were still missing.
Nugroho said human and material losses could be greater on the 300-kilometer coastline north of Palu, an area called Donggala, the closest to the epicenter of the earthquake.
Communications were “completely paralyzed” from Donggala, Nugroho said.
More than 300 000 people live there,” The Red Cross said in a statement, adding that its staff and volunteers were heading to the affected areas.
“The situation is dramatic and may well get worse,” she added.
On Saturday, the agency was widely criticized for failing to release information that a tsunami affected Palu, although agents said the waves had arrived while the alert was released.
An amateur video shared on social networks shows a man on the floor of a building frantically shouting to passers-by on the street that a tsunami is approaching.
A few minutes later, a wall of water fell on the coast, carrying with it buildings and cars.
Reuters could not immediately authenticate the images.
The earthquake and tsunami caused a general blackout that cut off all communications in Palu, so it was difficult for the authorities to coordinate rescue operations.
The army began to deliver aid by cargoo aircraft from Jakarta and other cities, according to authorities, but evacuees are still in great need for food and other basic necessities.
The city’s airport has reopened as part of rescue operations, but will remain closed until October.
President Joko Widodo was scheduled to visit Palu’s evacuation centers on Sunday.
Indonesia: the toll of the victims of the tsunami increases, there are more than 800 dead.
It's a tragedy.
Donggala’s World Vision staff arrived safely in Palu, where they found refuge in tarpaulin shelters established in the courtyard of their offices. Along the way, they discovered scenes of devastation, Doseba said.
“They told me they’d seen many houses destroyed,” he said.
It's a tragedy.
When the aid groups began to rescue the victims, some complained that very experienced foreign aid workers had not been allowed to travel to Palu.
According to Indonesian regulations, funds, supplies and foreign personnel can enter the territory only if the affected site is declared a national disaster zone.
This is not yet the case.
“This catastrophe is still considered regional,” said Aulia Arriani, spokeswoman for the Indonesian Red Cross.
“As soon as the government declares that this is a national disaster, international assistance can intervene, but we are still waiting.”
When the second night after Friday’s earthquake and tsunami fell on Palu, relatives of missing people still hoped for a miracle.
On Saturday, a little boy was found in the sewers.
On Sunday, rescuers released a woman buried under the rubble for two days, near her mother’s body.
Indonese paragliding coach Gendon Subandono had prepared two of the missing athletes for the Asian Games, which took place a few days earlier in Indonesia.
Others of his students were trapped in the Roa Roa Hotel, Mandagi added.
“As an elder in the paragliding discipline, I experienced my own dramages,” he explains.
In the hours following the announcement of the collapse of the Roa Roa Hotel in the paragliding community, Gendon recounted that he had desperately sent WhatsApp messages to athletes in Palu participating in the festival on the beach.
Her messages went unanswered.
“I don’t think they’ve ever received my messages.”
At Newport on the Levee, thieves steal $26,750 when reloading a vending machine
On Friday morning, thieves extorted $26,750 from a Brink’s agent reloading a vending machine in Newport on the Levee, according to a press release from the Newport Police Department.
The driver of the vehicle emptied a distributor in the entertainment complex and was preparing to power it, Inspector Dennis McCarthy said in a statement.
While he was busy, a man appeared behind Brink’s employee and stole a bag of money for the distributor.
Witnesses reportedly saw many suspects leave the scene, but the police did not specify the number of people involved in the incident.
Anyone with information about their identities is asked to contact the Newport police at 859-292-3680.
Kanye West : the rapper changes his name and becomes Ye
Rapper Kanye West is now calling Ye.
To announce the news on Twitter on Saturday, he wrote: “Formerly known as Kanye West.”
The 41-year-old West, who has been called Ye for some time, used the nickname as a title for his eighth album, released in June.
The rapper made this change just before heading to Saturday Night Live, where he is expected to present his new album, Yandhi.
His participation replaces that of Ariana Grande, who cancelled his arrival for “emotional reasons”, explained the creator of the show.
In addition to being the abbreviation of his current pseudo-profession, this nickname also has religious significance for West.
“I think ‘Ye’ is the most widely used word in the Bible, where it means ‘you,’” West explained earlier this year, citing the title of his album with host Big Boy.
“Then I’m you, I’m us.
I went from Kanye, who means “the only”, to Ye – the reflection of good, evil, confusion, everything.
“This album is a reflection of who we are.”
West isn’t the only known rapper to change his name.
Sean Combs was called Puff Daddy, P. Diddy or Diddy, but this year he announced his preference for Love and Brother Love.
JAY-Z, who has collaborated with West in the past, has also played on the hyphen and capital letters.
In Mexico, AMLO says it will never use military force against civilians.
Mexican President-elect Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador has vowed to never use military force against civilians, nearly fifty years after bloody retaliations by law enforcement against students.
Lopez Obrador vowed Saturday in Tlatelolco Square to “never use military force to repress the Mexican people.”
On October 2, 1968, the troops opened fire during a pacifist demonstration, killing 300 people as student movements took root throughout Latin America.
Lopez Obrador is committed to supporting Mexican youth by providing monthly student allowances and opening more free public universities.
According to him, unemployment and the lack of educational opportunities push young people to join criminal gangs.
The United States is expected to double funding for artificial intelligence
While China is increasingly active in the field of artificial intelligence, the U.S. is expected to double its funding dedicated to research in this sector, according to investor and artificial intelligence expert Kai-Fu Lee, who has worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
These statements come after several U.S. government actors made AI-related announcements, while an official AI strategy in the United States defaults.
In parallel, China presented its roadmap last year: it plans to become the leader in AI innovation by 2030.
“Doubling funding for AI research would be a good start, as all other countries are ahead of the United States, and we’re looking for the next big AI discovery.”
Doubling the funds could double the chances that the next key step in AI comes from the United States, Lee told CNBC in an interview this week.
Lee, whose book “AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order” was published this month by Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, is the CEO of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in one of China’s largest AI companies, Face++.
In the 1980’s, at Carnegie Mellon University, he worked on an AI system that beat the highest-ranked American Othello player. He then joined Microsoft Reasearch’s management team and became president of Google’s Chinese branch.
Lee has already participated in technology competitions organized by the U.S. government, such as the Robotics Challenge of the Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency, and asked when the next competition would take place, in order to identify visionaries.
According to Lee, U.S. researchers often have to work hard to get funding from the government.
“It’s not China that appropriates academic leaders, it’s the companies,” Lee says.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other tech companies have recruited major academic names to work on AI.
Lee added that changes in immigration policies could also support U.S. AI efforts.
“I think green cards should automatically be given to AI researchers,” he says.
China’s State Council unveiled its next-generation artificial intelligence development plan in July 2017.
“China’s National Foundation for Natural Sciences provides funding to members of academic institutions, just as the National Science Foundation and other government organizations distribute money to American researchers, but the quality of academic work in China is lower,” Lee explained.
Earlier in the year, the U.S. Department of Defense established a Joint Artificial Intelligence Center, which must involve industry and academy partners. The White House announced the formation of a Select Committee on Artificial Intelligence.
This month, DARPA announced a US$2 billion investment in an initiative called AI Next.
The NSF, meanwhile, currently invests more than US$100 million a year for AI research.
At the same time, U.S. law to establish a National Security Commission on Artificial Intelligence has not taken action for several months.
Macedonians take part in the referendum on their country’s name
On Sunday, the people of Macedonia made their voices heard in a referendum on changing their country’s name for the Republic of North Macedonia. This change would resolve a decades-old dispute with Greece, which had stalled Macedonia’s application for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece, of which one of the provinces is known as Macedonia, maintains that the name of its northern neighbor represents a right on its territory and has vetoed its entry into NATO and the EU.
In June, the two governments signed an agreement based on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents say the change would affect the ethical identity of Macedonia’s majority Slavic population.
President Gjorge Ivanov has announced that he will not vote in the referendum and that a boycott campaign has raised doubts about obtaining a minimum turnout of 50% required to validate the referendum.
“Are you for membership of NATO and the EU under the conditions of the agreement signed with Greece?”
According to supporters of the name change, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, this is the price of joining organisations such as the EU and NATO for Macedonia, a survivor of the collapse of Yugoslavia.
“I have come today to vote for the future of my country, for the youth of Macedonia, so that they can live freely under the aegis of the European Union. This means a safer life for all of us,” Olivera Georgijevska, 79, told Skopje.
Although the vote is not legally binding, enough members of parliament said they would follow the outcome of the vote to make it decisive.
Changing the name would require a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The State Electoral Commission announced that no irregularities had been detected at 1 p.m.
But turnout was only 16%, when it had reached 34% at the same time in the last parliamentary elections in 2016 which had mobilized 66% of registered voters at the end of the vote.
“I came to vote for my children. Our place is in Europe,” said Gjose Tanevski, 62, who voted in the capital, Skopje.
Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and his son Dushko participated in the referendum and voted for the name change, in order to open the voice for membership of NATO and the European Union in Strumica, Macedonia, on September 30, 2018.
In parliament, in Skopje, Vladimir Kavardarkov, 54, prepares a small stage and has the seats in front of tents installed by those who will boycott the referendum.
“We want to get into the EU and NATO, but we want to get into it through the big gate,” Kavadarkov said.
“We’re a poor country, but we have our dignity.
If they don’t want us as Macedonia, we can turn to China and Russia and participate in Eurasian integration.”
According to Prime Minister Zaev, NATO membership will allow Macedonia to benefit from the necessary investments. The unemployment rate in the country exceeds 20%.
“I think a large majority will be favorable, because more than 80% of our citizens want to join the EU and NATO,” Zaev said.
He added that a “yes” would be a “confirmation of our future.”
According to a poll published Monday by the Institute for Policy Research of Macedonia, between 30 and 43% of voters intend to participate in the referendum, which is lower than the expected turnout.
Another poll, organized by the Macedonian channel Telma, estimated participation at 57%.
70% of participants intending to vote stated that they would vote “yes”.
In order for the referendum to be valid, it takes 50% of the vote plus a vote.
The failure of the referendum would be the first blow to pro-western government policy since it took power in May last year.
Decisive action: Sergio Aguero of Manchester City makes his way through Brighton’s defence to score
Sergio Aguero and Raheem Sterling defeated Brighton’s defenders in Manchester and won Game 2-0 on Saturday at Etihad Stadium in Manchester.
Aguero’s goal, in the 65th minute, seemed to be the world’s simplest action for the player.
The Argentinian scorer received the ball in the middle of the field at the beginning of the action.
He ran between three defenders of Brighton, before plunging into the field.
Aguero was then surrounded by four green jerseys.
He bypassed a defender before ousting the others close to Brighton’s goal.
Then he passed the ball to Sterling on his left.
The English forward took the opportunity to return the ball to Aguero, who used his right foot to fool Brighton goaltender Mathew Ryan with a shot on the right side of the net.
“Aguero has some problems with his feet,” Pep Guardiola, the team’s coach, told reporters.
“We said he’d play 55, 60 minutes.
That is what happened.
We were lucky that he scored at that moment.”
But Sterling gave the Skyblues the initial advantage in the Premier League game.
The 29th minute.
Aguero received the ball directly on Brighton's field.
He sent it to the left at Leroy Sane.
Sane made a few touches before leading Sterling to the second post.
The front of the Skyblues sent the ball into the nets just before slipping off the field.
City will face Hoffenheim in the Champions Leagent group at 12h55 Tuesday at the Rhein-Neckar-Arena in Sinsheim, Germany.
Scherzer wants to play troublemakers against the Rockies
Since the Nationals team was eliminated from the play-off race, there was no reason to get started in a new game.
But Scherzer, very competitive, hopes to play Sunday against the Colorado Rockies, but only in the event of a playoff game for the Rockies, real legends against the Los Angeles Dodgers of the Western Division of the National League.
The Rockies won 5-2 against the Nationals on Friday night, but they’re still looking to secure their first-division title.
“Even if this game doesn’t have an issue, we want to tread the field in Denver, with this atmosphere, with the crowds, and against a team that will probably play at the highest level I’ll face this year.
“Why wouldn’t I want to participate?”
The Nationals still have to announce a launcher leaving for Sunday, but would be inclined to leave room for Scherzer, in this situation.
Scherzer, for whom it would be the 34th start, will be in training on Thursday and will launch on his usual rest day on Sunday.
The Washington right-hander is at 18-7 with an average of 2.53 and 300 withdrawals on catches in 220 steps and two-thirds this season.
Trump’s Interventions in West Virginia
The president alluded to the situation generated by his support of Brett Kavanaugh for the Supreme Court by talking about the importance of Republican participation in midterm elections.
“All of our actions will have an impact in November.
We’re five weeks away from one of the most important elections in our lives.
It’s a major issue. I’m not a candidate, but I’m more involved than ever. That’s why I’m fighting on all fronts for great candidates,” he said.
“You see this horrible radical group, really horrible, Democrats, you see it at work.
And they're determined to regain power by all means, see their wickedness.
They don’t care about who they’re injuring, who they’re crushing to take power and control. That’s what they want, power and control, and we’re not going to give them.”
Democrats are on a “resistance and obstruction” mission.
“You can see it over the last four days,” he added, before calling the Democrats “angry, mean and liar.”
He referred to the U.S. Senate Judiciary Committee and Democratic Senator Dianne Feinstein, leading to him being booed by the public.
“Do you remember his answer?”
Did you disclose the document?
Ha ha, of course.
No, no. I’m waiting for one. It was a very bad corporeal language, the worst I’ve ever seen.”
The Labour Party is no longer the great community it used to be.
It is not tolerant of those who express their ideas
When activists from the Momentum movement of my local party voted to censor me, it wasn’t a surprise.
After all, I’m the last of a line of Labour Party parliamentarians considered not to be welcome, all for expressing our ideas.
My parliamentary colleague Joan Ryan received the same treatment because she spoke out against anti-semitism.
As for me, I was censored because I disagreed with Jeremy Corbyn.
On the importance of a responsible economic policy, on national security, on Europe. Ironically, these are topics for which Jeremy himself disagreed with the previous leaders.
The notification for the Nottingham East Labour rally on Friday said: “We want the rally to be inclusive and productive.”
This is exactly what the Friday evening general meetings were during my eight years as a Labour Party parliamentarian.
Unfortunately, today this is not the case for many gatherings and the promise of softer and more respectful policies has been forgotten for a long time, if it has ever been taken into consideration.
It has become clear that contrary opinions are no longer tolerated within the party. Each opinion is judged on the basis of its acceptability by party leaders.
It started shortly after Jeremy took power. The colleagues with whom I thought to share the same political vision began to expect me to return my jacket and to take positions in contrast to my beliefs, whether on national security or the EU internal market.
Every time I spoke publicly, and no matter what I was saying, followed an abusive tirade on social media, calling for withdrawal, denouncing the center’s policies, telling me I shouldn’t be a member of the party.
It’s not just my experience.
Besides, I’m luckier than some of my colleagues, because the comments addressed to me were political in nature.
I admire the professionalism and determination of these colleagues, who face a surge of sexist or racist remarks every day, but who do not give up.
One of the most disappointing things in today’s policy is the normalization of different forms of abuse.
Last week, Jeremy Corbyn said that the Labour Party should encourage a culture of tolerance.
The reality is that we are no longer this great community and that the party restricts itself through motions of mistrust and changes to the rules of selection.
I’ve been given a lot of advice over the past two years: look down, don’t express my ideas so hard, and everything would go well.
But that’s not to do so I do politics.
I joined the Labour Party 32 years ago as a schoolboy, as the negligence of Thatcher’s government had caused the collapse of my classroom. Since then, I’ve wanted to work for better public services for those who need it most, whether as a local adviser or government minister.
I’ve never concealed my political ambitions, even in the last elections.
No one at Nottingham East could be disturbed by my political positions and disputes with current party officials.
All I’d like to say to those who defended the motion on Friday is that the country is moving towards a Brexit that’s going to affect homes, businesses and public services. I think it’s not really the time to waste time and energy by debating my loyalty to the party’s leader.
My only message is not for Nottingham Momentum, it is for my constituents, whether they are Labour or not: I am proud to serve you and I promise you that no threat of withdrawal or opportunism will prevent me from taking action for the causes I defend, which are your interests to all.
Chris Leslie is a member of Parliament for Nottingham East.
Ayr 38 - 17 Melrose: Ayr remains undefeated
Two late trials may have distorted the final result, but no doubt about it: Ayr deserved this triumph following the intense day game of the Scottish Rugby to XV Championship.
The team now occupies the top of the standings and becomes the only undefeated team in the championship.
In the end, it is the superiority of their defense and the way they are seized the opportunities that allowed them to open the score. Coach Peter Murchie can congratulate himself on this match.
“So far we’ve been tested. But we’re undefeated, so I have to think I’m happy!” he said.
Robyn Christie of the Melrose praised Ayr: “Congratulations to them, they have been more effective than us and knew how to seize the opportunities.”
Grant Anderson’s 14th-minute trial, transformed by Frazier Climo, put Ayr in a good position, but a yellow card for Scottish squadron Rory Hughes, released for the game by the Warriors, allowed Melrose to make the numbers speak.
Climo aggravated the situation for Ayr with a penalty, before, just at half-time. He scored and transformed a solo try to raise the score to 17-5 for Ayr at the break.
Melrose started well in the second half and Patrick Anderson’s try, transformed by Baggot, narrowed the gap to five points.
He ensued in a long wait because of a serious injury from Ruaridh Knott, who left the field on a stretcher. During the recovery, Ayr regained the lead with an attempt by Stafford McDowall transformed by Climo.
Blair Macpherson, acting captain of Ayr, then received a yellow card and, again, Melrose tried his luck with an untrapped trial of Bruce Colvine, who created palpable tension.
But the home team got back together and when Struan Hutchinson received a yellow card for tackling Climo when he didn’t have the ball, since the penalty’s touch, MacPherson hit the back of Ayr’s maul, which was progressing.
Climo transformed the trial, as almost every time since recovery, after Kyle Rowe rounded up David Armstrong’s box kick and senting winger Gregor Henry away for the team’s fifth try at home.
Still Game star launches into catering
Ford Kieran, the star of Still Game, has decided to make a career in catering. The actor has been appointed director of a licensed restaurant company.
The 56-year-old plays Jack Jarvis in the famous BBC show, which he writes and coanizes with his long-time partner on stage Greg Hemphill.
The duo have announced that the ninth series that is coming will be the final of the show. And Kiernan seems to be preparing for post-Craiglang.
According to official records, he is Director of Adriftmorn Limited.
The actor declined to comment on the story, though a Scottish Sun source revealed that Kiernan wanted to get involved in the “thriving dining environment” in Glasgow.
“The sea is ours”: Bolivia, blocked, hopes that the court will reopen the path to the Pacific
Sailors are patrolling an armoured boat in Naval Headquarters La Paz.
Public buildings have a blue flag like the ocean.
From Lake Titicaca to the Amazon, the naval bases display the following motorcycle: “The sea belongs to us by right.
Recovering it is a duty.”
Across blocked Bolivia, the memory of a coast lost to Chile during a bloody resource conflict in the 19th century is still present, as is the desire to sail on the Pacific Ocean, once again.
These hopes have not been so palpable for several decades. Bolivia is indeed awaiting a decision of the International Court of Justice, which will be delivered on October 1, after five years of deliberations.
“Bolivia has the will, a spirit of unity and serenity, and of course expects a positive result,” explains Roberto Calzadilla, a Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will follow the decision of the International Court of Justice on the big screen throughout the country, hoping that the court in The Hague will rule in favor of the Bolivian request. Bolivia hopes that, after decades of sporadic negotiations, Chile will be required to negotiate the agreement of a sovereign passage to the ocean to Bolivia.
Evo Morales, Bolivia’s charismatic indigenous president (who faces a controversial fight for re-election next year), also has a lot to say about Monday’s decision.
“We’re so close to being able to return to the Pacific Ocean,” he said at the end of August.
But some analysts believe that there is little chance that the court will rule in favour of Chile. And that little would change, even in the event of a positive decision.
The U.N. body based in the Netherlands does not have the power to grant Chilean territory, and stipulated that it would not determine the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that the International Court of Justice makes its decision six months after the final arguments shows that the case “was not complicated,” says Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert in international law.
And far from favoring the cause of Bolivia, the last four years may have even harmed him.
“The problem of access to the sea has been hijacked by the current Bolivian administration,” Zárate said.
Morales’ bellicous rhetoric undermined all of Chile’s remaining goodwill, she suggested.
Bolivia and Chile will continue to trade, but after that, it will be very difficult to hold talks.
The last exchange of ambassadors between the two countries took place in 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodríguez Veltzé, who represents Bolivia in The Hague, rejected the idea that the Court’s decision was abnormally swift.
On Monday will bring Bolivia “an extraordinary opportunity to start a new era of relations with Chile” and a chance to “end 139 years of disagreements with mutual benefits,” he said.
Calzadilla also disputed the fact that Morales – who remains to this day one of the most popular presidents in Latin America – used the maritime problem as a political crutch.
“Bolivia will never give up its right of access to the Pacific Ocean,” he added.
“The Court’s decision is an opportunity to see that we must get rid of the past.”
North Korea says that nuclear disarmament will only take place when it can trust the United States
Ri Yong Ho, North Korea’s foreign minister, said his country would not defeat its nuclear weapons until it trusted Washington.
Ri spoke at the United Nations General Assembly on Saturday.
He called on the United States to keep the promises made at a summit in Singapore between the leaders of the two rival countries.
He spoke at a time when U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo appears about to relaunch nuclear diplomacy at an impasse more than three months after the Singapore summit with Kim Jong Un, North Korea’s current leader.
Ri says it’s “chimerical” to believe that maintaining U.S. sanctions and opposition to a declaration that will end the Korean War will bend the North.
Washington is reluctant to accept the statement in the absence of prior significant disarmament measures by Pyongyang.
Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second summit.
But Pyongyang’s seriousness about renouncing an arsenal that the country sees as the only guarantee of its security is debated.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month, in order to prepare for a second meeting between Kim and Trump.
Parisian fashion shows reveal the latest trends when it comes to headgears, which may soon be available in your stores
If you want to add XXL pieces to your hat collection or protect yourself from the 100% sun, look no further.
Creators Valentino and Thom Browne presented their collections of delusional oversize hats at a PE19 show that honored Paris Fashion Week style.
Hats all more extravagant than each other invaded Instagram this summer, and the creators decided to incorporate their delusional pieces into the shows.
Valentino’s flagship piece was an eccentric beige hat adorned with a wide edge evoking a feather, which engulfed the heads of the mannequins.
The other oversize accessories included watermelons adorned with jewelry, a wizard hat and even pineapple. But these pieces were not designed to keep you warm.
Thom Browne has also presented a collection of original masks. Just in time for Halloween.
Most of the masks had their lips sewn and evoked Hannibal Lecter more than Haute-Couture.
A creation looked like diving equipment, with snorkel and glasses, while another was reminiscent of a cone of molten ice.
And if you follow the trends of fashion, then you’re lucky.
According to style observers, imposing hats could come all the way to your shopping streets.
.The XXL hats follow the wake of the “Bomba”, the straw hat on the wide edge that the stars are pulling out, from Rihanna to Emily Ratajkowski.
The cult brand that markets this hard-to-wear hat that flooded social networks presented another of its flagship creations on the podium: a straw beach bag almost as large as the swimming model who wore it.
The burnt orange rafia bag, adorned with fringes in rafia and a handful of white leather, was the centerpiece of Jacquemus’ PE19 La Riviera show during Paris Fashion Week.
Celebrity stylist Luke Armitage told FEMAIL: “I’m expecting to see the big hats and beach bags coming to the shops for next summer. The designer has made such an impression that it’s obvious that there’ll be demand for oversize accessories.”
John Edward: language skills are essential to the world’s citizens
Independent schools in Scotland maintain a record of academic excellence. This trend continued in 2018 with excellent new exam results reinforced by collective and individual success in sports, art, music and other community activities.
With more than 30 000 pupils throughout Scotland, these schools represented by the Scottish Council of Independent Schools (SCIS) are striving to provide the highest level of service to pupils and their parents.
Independent schools work to prepare their pupils for higher education, the careers they have chosen and their place as citizens of the world.
As a sector of education able to design and implement a tailor-made school curriculum, we find that contemporary languages remain popular and desired subjects in schools.
Nelson Mandela said: “If you speak to a man in a language he understands, you speak at his head.
“If you speak to him in his language, you speak to his heart.”
It reminds us that we can’t rely on English to build relationships and build trust with people in other countries.
If we look at the excellent results of the exams this year, we see that the languages are at the top of the rankings, with the most important success rates in independent schools.
In total, 68% of pupils who studied foreign languages received the highest score, A.
The data, collected from the 74 SCIS member schools, show that 72% of pupils received the highest grade, A, in Mandarin, as well as the 72% of pupils who studied German, the 69% of pupils who studied French and the 63% of students who studied Spanish.
This shows that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as absolutely essential skills for children and youth in the future.
From now on, languages as subjects are organized as are science, technology, engineering and mathematics in the curricula of independent schools and other schools.
A survey by the UK Commission for Employment and Skills carried out in 2014 reveals that among the difficulties employers face in recruiting candidates, 17% are linked to insufficient language skills.
Language skills are therefore becoming increasingly vital to preparing young people for their future careers.
With more job offers requiring languages, these skills are essential in our globalized world.
Regardless of their career choice, someone who speaks a second language will benefit from a real advantage in the future thanks to this sustainable skill.
Being able to communicate directly with strangers will automatically place the multilingual person in front of their competitors.
According to a 2013 YouGov survey of 4,0000 adults living in the United Kingdom, 75% of them did not have sufficient control of a foreign language to hold a conversation. French was the only language spoken by a double-digit percentage (15%).
That’s why investing in language learning is important for children today.
By speaking several languages, including those in emerging economies, children will have a better chance of finding a rewarding job.
In Scotland, each school will teach different languages.
Many of them will focus on classical contemporary languages, while others will teach languages considered important to the United Kingdom in the perspective of 2020, including Mandarin or Japanese.
Regardless of your child’s preferences, several languages will be accessible in independent schools, with specialist staff in the field.
Scottish independent schools want to offer a learning environment that will prepare children and give them the skills they need to succeed, no matter what the future holds.
It is obvious that at present, in a global business environment, languages remain of crucial importance for the future of the country, and this must be reflected in education.
Modern languages must be regarded as “skills of international communication”.
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence to Scottish youth.
We have to do it.
John Edward is director of the Scottish Council of Independent Schools
LeBron will debut with the Lakers on Saturday in San Diego
The expectation is almost over for fans watching LeBron James debut at the Los Angeles Lakers.
Lakers coach Luke Walton said James will play the Denver Nuggets in San Diego on Sunday in pre-season.
We don’t know how many minutes he’ll play yet.
“More than one, less than 48,” Walton said on the Lakers’ official website.
Lakers reporter Mike Trudell tweeted that James would probably play for a few limited minutes.
After training, earlier in the week, James were asked his plans for the pre-season program, which includes six games.
“At this point in my career, I don’t need pre-season games to be ready.”
Trump's Campaign in West Virginia (YouTube Channel)
President Donald Trump is starting a series of campaign rallies tonight in Wheeling, West Virginia.
Other stopovers, especially in states such as Tennessee and Mississippi where the president is popular, are planned.
With the Supreme Court vacancy confirmation vote being postponed, Trump is seeking support for the upcoming midterm elections and preventing Republicans from losing control of Congress during the November vote.
At what time is Trump’s meeting in West Virginia tonight and how to watch it online?
Trump’s meeting in Wheeling, West Virginia, is scheduled for 7:00 p.m
You can follow him online below, live on YouTube.
Trump is expected to address Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh’s hearings, accused of sexual assault, which took place this week. Kavanaugh’s confirmation vote in the Senate is expected to be postponed until a week during the FBI’s investigation.
But this series of meetings aims above all to help Republicans gain traction ahead of the November elections.
According to Reuters, the North American president said in his campaign that the five meetings were intended to “encourage volunteers and supporters as Republicans try to protect and expand the majorities they hold in the Senate and the House of Representatives.”
“Congression’s control is such an essential part of its program that the president will travel to as many states as possible at the beginning of the busy election campaign,” a spokesman for the Trump team told Reuters.
According to the West Virginia Metro News, tonight’s meeting, scheduled at Wheeling’s Wesbanco Arena, could reunify fans from “Ohio and Pennsylvania, and attract attention from Pittsburgh media.”
On Saturday, it will be Trump’s second month-on-month visit to West Virginia, a state he won in 2016 by more than 40 percentage points.
Trump is trying to help Patrick Morrisey, a Republican candidate for the Senate of West Virginia, who is lagging behind in the polls.
“It’s not a good sign for Morrisey that the president has to come and try to relaunch him in the polls,” said Simon Haeder, a political scientist at West Virginia University.
Ryder Cup 2018: U.S. team ready to fight to keep hope ahead of Sunday’s singles
After three singles, Saturday afternoon’s foursomes could well represent what this Ryder Cup lacked.
The momentum pendulum movement is an invented sporting concept from scratch but in which players sincerely believe, and more than ever in tournaments like this.
So where would they set the momentum now?
“They’re leading six points ahead and now four, so it’s giving us a bit of wings,” Spieth said as he left for the day.
Europe has the advantage, of course, with four points ahead and twelve more to play.
Americans, according to Spieth, have the feeling that they have a little wind in their sails and have every reason to be encouraged, including by Spieth and Justin Thomas who have played together all day and can claim to have each scored three out of four points.
Spieth has been terribly effective from tee to green and sets an example.
The energetic shouts of joy intensified as his game progressed. A crucial putt allowed him to equalize in the fourth game while he and Thomas were leaded twice after two holes.
His putt, thanks to which they won the game at the 15th hole, aroused a similar cry, from those who tell you that he still believes the American team still in contention.
“You really have to stay determined and focus on your game,” Spieth said.
That’s all there’s left for each of these players now.
18 holes to stand out.
The only players to have scored more points than Spieth and Thomas in the past two days are Francesco Molinari and Tommy Fleetwood, as evidenced by Ryder Cup history.
The strange but adorable European pair has won four games out of four and has no right to error.
Not only was “Moliwood” the only one not to perform a bogey on Saturday afternoon, but they also escaped Saturday morning, Friday afternoon and the last nine holes on Friday morning.
This series, the way their energy seems to flow between them and the delusional crowd designate them as the players to beat on Sunday, and there is no player more popular than Fleetwood or Molinari to delight a potential European victory while the sun sets on the National Golf.
Preferably, both at the same time at different holes.
However, talking about European glory remains premature.
Bubba Watson and Webb Simpson quickly beat Sergio Garcia, hero of the fourballs of the morning, as he teamed with Alex Noren.
A bogey and two doubles on the first nine shots sent the Spaniard and Swede into a hole from which they could never emerge.
However, Sunday is not there to help you.
Fourballs and foursomes are so fascinating to observe closely because of the interactions between the pairs, the advice they give and those they don’t give, and how a strategy can tilt in the blink of an eye.
Europe has played better as a team so far and took a considerable lead during the final day, but this foursome session has also proven that the Americans have the courage to fight, which some doubted, especially in the country.
Europe Leads 10-6 on Last Day of the Ryder Cup
Europe will enjoy a solid advantage in the final day of the Ryder Cup after winning 10-6 over the United States in Saturday’s fourballs and foursomes.
Inspired duo Tommy Fleetwood and Francesco Molinari led the charge with two wins on a distressed Tiger Woods, which allowed them to score four points so far.
Determined to keep the title she lost in Hazeltine two years ago, Thomas Bjorn’s European team made the difference by winning 3-1 in the morning’s fourballs, facing a messy U.S. team.
The U.S. demonstrated greater resistance in the foursomes and achieved two victories, but it failed to address the gap.
Jim Furyk’s men have to get eight points out of 12 on Sunday during the singles to keep the trophy.
Fleetwood is the first European rookie to win four points in a row, while Molinari and him, nicknamed “Molliwood” after a smashing weekend, are only the second pair in Ryder Cup history to score four points in their first four games.
After crushing Woods and Patrick Reed during fourballs, they beat the pair consisting of American beginner Bryson Dechambeau and a Woods already tired by an even more categorical 5 and 4.
Woods, who dragged on during Saturday’s two games, shone a few times, but has now lost 19 of his 29 games in fourballs and foursomes, and seven in a row.
Justin Rose, well rested for the morning fourballs, teamed with Henrik Stenson in the foursomes, winning 2-1 against Dustin Johnson and Brooks Koepka, respectively number one and three in the world rankings.
Europe was not spared during the tournament held in the south-west of Paris, under a cool but clear weather.
Three-time winners Jordan Spieth and Justin Tho Thomas set the benchmark for the Americans by scoring two points on Saturday.
They scored a well-deserved 2-1 victory over Spain’s Jon Rahm and Ian Poulter in the fourballs and again beat Poulter and Rory McIlroy 4-3 in the foursomes, with these having lost the first two holes.
Only twice in the history of the Ryder Cup, a team has recovered from a gap of four single points, although Furyk’s team, which held the wreath, only has to draw to keep its trophy.
After being second for two days, however, they may not recover from a counterattack on Sunday.
North Korea declares that disarmament cannot in “any case” be done unilaterally without confidence.
On Saturday, the North-Korean foreign minister told the United Nations that the continuation of sanctions reinforced his distrust of the United States and that it would be impossible for his country to unilaterally renounce its nuclear weapons in these circumstances.
At the Global Body’s Annual General Assembly, Ri Yong Ho claimed that last year North Korea took “important measures of goodwill,” such as endinging such as stopping nuclear and missile testing, dismantling the nuclear testing site and its commitment not to proliferate nuclear weapons and technology
“However, we do not see any corresponding response from the United States,” he said
In the absence of trust in the United States, we will fear for our national security and, in such circumstances, it is excluded that we are the first to unilaterally disarm ourselves.”
While reiterating North Korea’s well-known reproaches over Washington’s opposition to a “stage” approach to denuclearization under which North Korea would be rewarded if it was gradually taking action, its statement has proved important that it does not reject the idea of unilateral denuclearization as Pyongyang had done in the past.
Ri referred to a joint statement by Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump, made on June 12 in Singapore at the first-ever summit between a sitting U.S. president and a North Korean leader, when Kim pledged to work for a “denuclearization of the Korean peninsula” while Trump promised to guarantee North Korea’s security.
North Korea seeks to formally end the Korean War (1950-1953), but the U.S. said Pyongyang must first give up its nuclear weapons.
Washington has also denied requests to loosen harsh international sanctions against North Korea.
The U.S. insists that North Korea first deneuclarize and increase pressure by imposing sanctions to achieve their goal coercitively, even opposing “the end-of-war declaration.”
“Those who think that these sanctions can make us bend do not know us.
“The problem is that maintaining sanctions increases our mistrust.”
Ri made no comments about the second summit between Kim and Trump highlighted at the United Nations by the U.S. leader earlier this week.
The minister instead pointed out three meetings between Kim and South Korean leader Moon Jae-in over the past five months, and added: “If the party concerned by the issue of denuclearization was South Korea and not the United States, the denuclearization of the Korean peninsula would not be at such an impasse.”
Nevertheless, the tone of Ri’s discourse was radically different from last year’s, when he told the United Nations General Assembly that it was inevitable to spot North Korean missiles on North American territory after “evil” President Trump called Kim a “missile man” on a suicide mission.
Trump, who last year threatened the United Nations to “totally destroy” North Korea, praised this year the courage with which Kim implemented disarmament measures, but added that there is still a lot to be done and that the sanctions would not be lifted until North Korea disarmed.
On Wednesday, Trump said that no deadline has been set. “It can take two years, three years or five months; it doesn’t matter,” he said.
China and Russia argue that the United Nations Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures adopted.
However, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo told the United Nations Security Council on Thursday that: “The implementation of Security Council sanctions must be firmly and systematically maintained until total, definitive and verified denuclearization.”
The Security Council has unanimously tightened sanctions against North Korea since 2006 to stifle the funding of Pyongyang’s nuclear programs and ballistic missiles.
Pompeo met Ri on the sidelines of the United Nations General Assembly and then announced that he would return to Pyongyang next month to prepare for a second summit.
This year, Pompeo has already traveled to North Korea three times, but his last trip was somewhat disastrous.
In July, he left Pyongyang claiming that progress had been made but, in the hours that followed, North Korea denounced its “gangster methods.”
In a meeting with Moon this month, North Korea pledged to dismantle a missile site as well as a nuclear complex if the U.S. took “equivalent measures.”
According to the South Korean president, Kim is calling for Trump’s promised security guarantees in Singapore, and efforts to normalize relations with Washington.
Harvard : students take resting classes
This year, Harvard University has set up a new course that allows all undergraduate students to sleep more and struggles with the growing macho tendency to study using caffeine throughout the night.
A professor has noticed that most students from the world’s best university don’t know how to take care of them
This course, designed by Charles Czeisler, professor of sleep medicine at Harvard Medical School and a specialist at Brigham and Women’s Hospital, according to him, is the first of its kind in the United States.
It is after giving a lecture on the effects of sleep deprivation on learning that the idea came to him
In the end, a student approached and said, "Why is this only said in the final year?"
She claimed that no one had ever told her about the importance of sleep, which surprised me,” he told The Telegraph.
Delivered for the first time this year, this course explains to students the need to adopt good sleep habits to improve their academic and physical performance, as well as their overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, professor of psychiatry at Harvard Medical School and managing director of health services at the university, said the university had decided to introduce the course after finding that students suffered from a severe lack of sleep during the week.
The course time consists of various interactive exercises.
One of the sections presents the image of a dormitory containing different objects (coffee cups, curtains, sneakers, books...) on which students click to know the effects of caffeine and light, as well as the impact of lack of sleep on their physical services and the importance of integrating a routine before bedtime.
In another section, participants learn how lack of long-term sleep can increase the risk of heart attack, stroke, depression and cancer.
A map of the campus with interactive icons then invites participants to reflect on their daily routine.
“We know it won’t instantly change student behavior.
However, in our opinion, they have the right to know this information – in the same way that you have the right to know what are the health effects of smoking,” added Professor Czeisler.
According to him, the culture of the pride of “passing a white night” still exists, and he adds that modern technology and the ever-increasing pressure on students make the lack of sleep a growing problem.
Having sufficient and good quality sleep should be the “secret weapon” of students to combat stress, exhaustion and anxiety, he claimed – even to avoid gaining weight, because the lack of sleep puts the brain into a mode of inanition, making them constantly hungry.
Raymond So, a 19-year-old student of Chemistry and Physical Biology, helped Professor Czeisler design this course after being one of his students last year during his first year at Harvard.
He claimed that this course had opened his eyes and pushed him to insist that he be offered to the entire campus.
He hopes that the next step will be to offer all postgraduate students the opportunity to take a similar program of study before joining the reputable institution.
Professor Czeisler recommends that students provide an alarm for both bedtime and wake-up, and be aware of the harmful effects of “blue light” emitted by electronic screens and LED light, which can disrupt their circadian rhythm and lead to sleeping disorders.
Livingston 1-0 Rangers : Menga's goal makes Gerrard's men fall
The Rangers suffered yet another blow to the outside, with Dolly Menga’s attack leading Steven Gerrard’s disarticulated team in Livingston into a 1-0 loss.
Ibrox’s team hoped to record their first on-the-date win since their triumphant 4-1 in February at St Johnstone, but Gary Holt’s team gave Gerrard their second loss in 18 games as a coach, leaving his team adrift, eight points behind Ladbroke Premiership’s Hearts.
Menga hit seven minutes before half-time and the Rangers team, running out of inspiration, failed to equalize.
As the Rangers now fall to sixth, Livingston climbs third, just behind Hibernian on the goal difference.
And other difficulties could be felt for the Rangers, the referee Calum Spence having had to be healed for a head injury following the throwing of an object from the opposite side.
Gerrard made eight changes in the team, which swept Ayr in the Betfred Cup semi-finals.
Holt, meanwhile, chose the same Livi 11 which snatched a point from the Hearts last week and would have been delighted to see that his well-trained team suffocated his opponents in each round.
The Rangers may have mastered possession of the ball, but Livingston did more.
They should have scored two minutes, when Menga’s first shot Scott Pittman on Allan McGregor’s goal, but the midfielder missed his chance.
Keaghan Jacobs then fired a powerful shot towards captain Craig Halkett, but defending teammate Alan Lithgow could only reach the second post
The Rangers managed to take control, but there seemed to be more hope than faith in their game in the last third.
Alfredo Morelos certainly felt that he should have benefited from a penalty after the free-kick while Steven Lawless and he collided but referee Steven Thomson dismissed the Colombian’s demands.
The Rangers took advantage of only two shots in the first period, but Ibrox’s former goaltender, Liam Kelly, was barely disturbed by Lassana Coulibaly and an Ovie Ejaria strike.
If Livi’s opening score in the 34th minute was written against the course of the game, no one can deny that they deserved it from their hard work.
Once again, the Rangers failed in the face of Jacobs’ stopped kick.
Scott Arfield didn’t react when Declan Gallagher handed the ball over to Scott Robinson, who kept his cool to choose Menga for an ending all with simplicity.
Gerrard reacted at half-time by replacing Coulibaly with Ryan Kent and the exchange almost had an immediate impact when the winger showed up ahead of Morelos, but the impressive Kelly rushed to block.
Livingston still continued to push visitors to play exactly the type of game they like, with Lithgow and Halkett sweeping long balls.
Holt’s team could have taken the lead in the final moments, but McGregor did well to push Jacobs back before Lithgow missed the goal.
Rangers substitute Glenn Middleton once again demanded a penalty at the end of the game while he was struggling with Jacobs, but Thomson ignored him again.
Almanach : The inventor of the Geiger counter
And now, a page from our Almanach “Sunday Morning”: September 30, 1882, 136 years ago today, and THE COUNTER TURNS... the future physicist Johannes Wilhelm “Hans” Geiger, in Germany was born.
Geiger developed a method for detecting and measuring radioactivity; an invention that subsequently gave birth to the device known as the Geiger counter.
Pillar of science since then, the Geiger counter has also become fundamental in pop culture, as can be seen in the film “Bells of Coronado” of 1950, with Roy Rogers and Dale Evans, two unlikely scientists:
A man: “What is this?”
Rogers: “It’s a Geiger counter, we use it to look for radioactive minerals, like uranium.
Thanks to these headphones, we can really hear the effects of atoms released into the minerals through radioactivity.”
Evans: “Say, it’s sure it’s jumping there!”
“Hans” Geiger died in 1945, a few days before his 63rd birthday.
However, the invention that bears his name remains.
A new cancer vaccine allows the immune system to “detect” cancer cells
A new cancer vaccine allows the immune system to “detect” cancer cells and kill them
As part of the treatment, the vaccine tells the immune system how to recognize cancer cells
This technique consists of extracting immune cells from a patient and modifying them in the laboratory
They can then “detect” a protein that is common to many cancers, and are then reinjected
An experimental vaccine shows encouraging results in patients with different types of cancer.
A woman who received the vaccine, whose goal is to teach the immune system to recognize cancer cells, has seen her ovarian cancer disappear for more than 18 months.
The method consists of extracting immune cells from a patient and modifying them in the laboratory, so that they can detect the HER2 protein, common to many cancers, and finally to reinject them.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the U.S. National Cancer Institute based in Bethesda, Maryland, said: “Our results indicate that we have a very promising vaccine.”
According to Professor Berzofsky, the HER2 protein “causes an increase in several types of cancers”, including breast, ovarian, lung and colorectal cancer.
A similar approach, taking immune cells to “teach” them to target cancer cells, has been proven in the treatment of a type of leukemia.
Capped with a MAGA cap, Kanye West embarked on a Pro-Trump diatribe after his time at SNL.
It didn’t go well
Kanye West got booed on the set after an unleashed performance in which he praised U.S. President Donald Trump on a Saturday Night Live and announced that he would run for office in 2020.
Following his third track, Ghost Town, which he sang while wearing a Make America Great Again cap, West attacked Democrats and reiterated his support for Trump.
“A lot of times, I’m talking to white people and they say, ‘How can you love Trump? He’s racist!’
"Well, if I'm worried about racism, I'd've left America a long ago," he said.
SNL kicked out with a sketch by Matt Damon in which Hollywood star mocks Brett Kavanaugh’s testimony before the Senate Judiciary Committee about Christine Blasey Ford’s sexual assault allegations.
Although it wasn’t streamed, the recording of West’s diatribe was relayed on social media by comedian Chris Rock.
We don't know if Rock intended to make fun of West with this publication.
In addition, West complained to the public that he had spent a bad quarter of an hour behind the scenes because of his cap.
“They put pressure on me.
They told me not to come with this cap on their heads.
They put pressure on me.
And then they say I’m in an abyss,” he said, according to the Washington Examiner.
West continued: “Do you want to see him, the abyss?” and claimed that he would wear his superman’s cloak, because no one can dictate his actions. You want the world to progress?
Try love.”
His comments lured boos at least twice from the public and SNL members seemed embarrassed, reports Variety. A person on the scene told the magazine: “A death silence fell on the entire plateau.”
West had been called to replace singer Ariana Grande at the last minute, whose former boyfriend, rapper Mac Miller, died a few days earlier.
West knocked out a lot of people by singing the song I Love it in a Perrier bottle costume.
West received the support of the leader of the conservative group TPUSA, Candace Turner, who published the following tweet: “To one of the most courageous minds: THANK YOU FOR HAVING KEPT HEAD TO THE CROWD.”
But Karen Hunter tweeted that West was simply “faithful to himself, and it’s absolutely wonderful.”
“However, I choose not to reward someone (by buying their music, clothes, or supporting their art) who, in my opinion, adopts and pours an ideology harmful to my community.
He's free.
And so are we,” she added.
Prior to the show, the rapper had announced on Twitter that he had changed his name and said he was now “officially known as Kanye West.”
He’s not the first artist to change his name and follows in the footsteps of Diddy, also known as Puff Daddy, Puffy and P Diddy.
Rapper Snoop Dogg bears the name of Snoop Lion and of course the legendary fire Prince, which changed its name to a symbol, then that of the artist previously known as Prince.
Belfast: Indictment of attempted murder after stabbing in a restaurant
A 45-year-old man is charged with attempted murder after a man was stabbed last Friday at a restaurant east of Belfast.
According to police, the incident occurred in Ballyhackamore.
The defendant is expected to appear before the Belfast Court of First Instance on Monday.
The charges will be examined by the Public Prosecutor’s Office.
Kit Harington, the star of the Game of Thrones series, attacks toxic masculinity
Kit Harington is known for his fine-bladed role, Jon Snow in Game of Thrones, HBO’s violent Fantastic Medieval Series.
But the 31-year-old actor attacked the stereotype of the macho hero, claiming that this type of role on the screen often causes young boys to pose as hard-boiled to enforce themselves.
In an interview with the Sunday Times Culture, Kit said “something is wrong” and addresses the issue of toxic masculinity at #MeToo.
Kit, who recently married 31-year-old Game of Thrones co-wired Rose Leslie, confessed he was “strong” to tackle this issue.
“Personally, I deeply think we’ve abused masculinity.”
“What teachings have we given to young men who become adults, in relation to the problem that arises now?”
According to Kit, television could be partly responsible because of the increase in toxic masculinity because of its very male characters.
He continued: “Which behavior is innate, what behavior was instilled?
What does television and the street teach young boys who encourage them to think that they have to behave in this way to be a man?
I believe this is really one of the fundamental questions of our time – how can we change that?
There’s obviously something wrong.”
In the interview, he also confessed that he wouldn’t make prologues or sequels when the series ended next summer, claiming to be done with “battlefields and horses.”
Starting in November, Kit will star in a cover of Sam Shepard’s True West, which tells the story of a film producer and his brother, a thief.
Recently, the actor revealed that the meeting with his wife Rose was the best thing for him that happened to him in Game of Thrones.
“I met my wife in this series, so she offered me my future family and my life from now on,” he said.
Rose played Ygritte, Kit’s lover, Jon Snow, in the fantasy series that won an Emmy.
The couple got married in June 2018 in Scotland at Leslie’s family estate.
HIV/AIDS: China reports 14% increase in new cases
China reported a 14% increase in the number of people living with HIV/AIDS.
According to the Chinese health authorities, more than 820 000 people are affected.
Nearly 40 000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018.
Most patients have been infected during sexual intercourse, which is a new fact.
Historically, the virus has spread rapidly in some parts of the country as a result of transfusions of contaminated blood.
However, the number of people infected with HIV in this way has been virtually reduced to zero, Chinese health authorities said at a conference in Yunnan province.
However, year-over-year, the number of HIV-positive people living with AIDS in China has increased by 100 000 people.
In China’s LGBT community, transmission of HIV through sexual intercourse is a serious problem.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against the LGBT community remains widespread.
Due to the country’s conservative values, studies estimate that 70% to 90% of gay men end up marrying women.
Many cases of transmission are due to inadequate sexual protections.
As part of its efforts to combat this problem, the Chinese government has promised universal access to HIV medicines since 2003.
Maxine Waters denies that a GOP employee has leaked data relating to senators, and denounces “dangerous lies” and “conspiracy theories.”
U.S. Republican Maxine Waters denounced allegations that a member of her team posted personal information about three Republican senators on Wikipedia pages dedicated to lawmakers.
The Los Angeles Democrat claimed that these statements were backed by “far-right” experts and websites.
“Lies, lies, and infamous lies,” Waters said in a statement on Twitter.
It would appear that the information leaked included the addresses and telephone numbers of U.S. Senators Lindsey Graham of South Carolina, Mike Lee and Orrin Hatch, both from Utah.
The information was posted online Thursday by an unknown on Capitol Hill during the Senate session on allegations of sexual assault against Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh.
The leak occurred some time after the three senators questioned Kavanaugh.
According to the Hill, conservative sites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState indicated that the IP address identifying the source of the messages was associated with the Waters office and leaked information from a Waters staff member.
According to Waters, “this unfounded allegation is completely false and an absolute lie.”
“The member of my team, whose identity, personal information and security have been compromised as a result of these fraudulent and false allegations, is in no way responsible for leaking this information.”
“This fallacious allegation is completely false and a real lie.”
Waters’ statement quickly sparked online criticism, including from Ari Fleischer, the former White House press secretary.
“This denial is anger,” he wrote.
“This indicates that it does not have the necessary disposition to be a member of Congress.
When a person is accused of something they haven’t done, they shouldn’t be angry.
It doesn't have to be provocative.
It must not call into question the accuser’s motives.
It must remain calm and serene.”
Fleischer seemed to be comparing Waters’s reaction to the Democrats’ criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, accused of appearing too angry at Thursday’s hearing.
Omar Navarro, a Republican candidate for the Waters headquarters in the midterm elections, also expressed his reflections on Twitter.
“If that’s true, it’s huge,” he wrote on Twitter.
In his statement, Waters said his office had alerted “the relevant law enforcement authorities and services” about these fraudulent claims.
“We will ensure that the culprits are unmasked,” she added, “and that they are held accountable for all their destructive and dangerous actions for all members of my team.”
Johnny English struck again. Parody of Rowan Atkinson
It has now become a tradition to look for the reach of Brexit in any new British film and it seems to apply to this revival of the action comedy Johnny English, which began in 2003 with Johnny English and resurfaced in 2011 with Johnny English, the return.
Willing self-satire on the question of the evidence of our insignificance represent our country’s new opportunity to export?
In any case, the incompetent Johnny English, with wide eyes and rubber face, saw his license renewed for the second time in a confusing context - this name indicating above all that it is a great comedic creation designed for a non-Anglophone film audience.
Of course, he is the stupid secret agent who, despite his dubious attempts as a glamorous charmer, has a bit of Clouseau, a hint of Mr. Bean and a good dose of the guy who performs a small note in The Fire Carts at the opening ceremony of the London 2012 Olympic Games.
Originally, he was also inspired by the traveler and the man of international mystery that Atkinson once starred in Barclaycard television commercials, now forgotten, leaving the chaos behind.
The last Johnny English has one or two friendly moments.
I liked Johnny English approaching a helicopter while wearing medieval armor and the rotor blades struck briefly against his helmet.
Atkinson’s talent for physical comedy is highlighted, although humor is rather insufficient and strangely superfluous, especially at a time when “serious” licenses like 007 and Mission impossible compose themselves in confidence with comedy.
Humory seems more oriented towards children than adults and, for me, Johnny English’s wacky misadventures are less creative and less targeted than Atkinson’s mute movie gags in Bean’s character.
The ever-topical premise is that Britain has serious problems.
A hacker infiltrated the super-secret web network of British spies, revealing the identities of all British agents on the ground, to the dismay of serving agents - an unfortunately small role for Kevin Eldon.
It’s the gout that overflows the vase for a Prime Minister who is a pompous character on the verge of crisis and who is already suffering from political unpopularity: Emma Thompson gives the best of herself with this quasi-Teresa-May character, but there’s not much to put herself under the tooth in the script.
Her intelligence advisers inform her that since every active spy has been compromised, she’ll have to get someone out of retirement.
This means disturbing Johnny English himself, who is now employed in a chic institution as a schoolmaster, but who secretly gives lessons on how to become a secret agent: a few beautiful farces here, as English offers a spy academy like Rock Academy.
English is brought back to Whitehall for an emergency briefing and finds Bough, his former long-term partner, once again encamped by Ben Miller.
Bough is now married, attached to a submarine commander, a fun hockey stick role in which Vicki Pepperdine is somewhat wasted.
So, Batman and Robin, who are creating big trouble for His Majesty’s secret services, go back to action and meet Olga Kurylenko, the beautiful femme fatale Ophelia Bulletova.
In the meantime, the Prime Minister falls dangerously under the grip of the charismatic technological billionaire who claims to be able to solve Britain’s computer problems: the sinister Jason Volta, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough launch their odyssey of wacky jokes: under the disguise of waiters, they set fire to a bright French restaurant; they create chaos by sneaking on Volta’s luxurious sailboat; and English triggers a real anarchy when he tries, with a virtual reality helmet, to familiarize themselves with the interior of Volta’s house.
All stops are assured for this last sequence, but despite everything, there are a lot of things for children in all this.
It was a rather moderate film.
I couldn’t help but think, as with Johnny English’s other films: can’t the British film industry offer Rowan Atkinson a role that will really value his talent?
The Labour Party denies having drawn up a plan so that the British can work four days but that they are paid for five days
Jeremy Corbyn’s Labour Party must consider a radical plan that will allow the British to work four days a week – but to be paid for five.
Apparently, the party wants business leaders to benefit workers from the savings generated by artificial intelligence (AI) by giving them an extra day off.
This would allow employees to enjoy a three-day weekend, while continuing to receive the same salary.
According to some sources, the idea would be consistent with the party’s economic agenda and plans to guide the country in favour of workers.
The four-day week has been approved by the Trade Union Congress as a way for workers to take advantage of economic changes.
An important source within the Labour Party told the Sunday Times: “A policy review is expected to be announced before the end of the year.”
“It won’t happen overnight, but a four-day working week is a goal that goes hand in hand with the party’s approach to rebalancing the economy in favor of workers as well as its overall industrial strategy.”
The Labour Party would not be the first to support this idea, since the Green Party was committed to a four-day working week during its 2017 general election campaign.
However, this aspiration is currently not supported by the Labour Party as a whole.
A spokesman for the Labour Party said: “A four-day working week does not constitute a party policy and is not considered by it.”
Deputy Chancellor John McDonnell took advantage of last week’s Labour conference to give details of his vision of a socialist revolution in the economy.
McDonnell expressed his determination to regain power in the hands of “anonymous administrators” and “profiters” in utilities.
The plans of the Alternate Chancellor also provide that the current shareholders of the water companies may not recover their full stake since the Labour government could make “deductions” due to presumed driving gaps.
It also confirmed its plan to integrate workers into corporate boards as well as the creation of an inclusive shareholder fund that will contribute 10% of the capital of private companies to employees, which will pocket up to £500 of dividends per year.
Lindsey Graham and John Kennedy entrust “60 Minutes” if the FBI’s investigation into Kavanaugh could change their minds.
The final vote on Kavanaugh’s appointment to the Supreme Court has been postponed by at least a week following the FBI’s investigation into the charges against him and raises the question of whether the Bureau’s findings could prompt Republican senators to withdraw their support.
In an interview on Sunday, 60-Minute Program Correspondent Scott Pelley asked Republican Senators John Kennedy and Lindsey Graham whether the FBI could discover anything that would cause them to change their minds.
Kennedy was more open than his colleague from South Carolina.
"Obviously," Kennedy said.
“Going to the hearing, I said I spoke to Judge Kavanaugh.
I called him after it happened, when these allegations were made, and I asked him, “Did you do that?”
He was resolved, determined, unequivocal.”
However, Graham’s vote seems set in stone.
“I made my decision about Brett Kavanaugh and we need an explosive accusation,” he said.
“Dr. Ford, I don’t know what happened, but I know this: Brett denied it categorically,” Graham added, referring to Christine Blasey Ford.
“And the people she names couldn’t verify it.
It’s a 36-year-old affair.
I don’t see anything new that changes.”
What is the Global Citizen Festival doing something to reduce poverty?
This Saturday, New York will host the Global Citizen Festival, an annual musical event with a very impressive list of stars and invested with an equally impressive mission: to end poverty in the world.
The Global Citizen Festival, now in its seventh edition, will see tens of thousands of people rush to the Great Lawn of Central Park in order not only to take advantage of the performance of artists such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to raise awareness of the festival’s real goal of ending extreme poverty by 2030.
The Global Citizen Festival, the first edition of which took place in 2012, is a Global Poverty Project expansion, an international advocacy group that hopes to eradicate poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting it.
In order to receive a free ticket for the event (unless you’re willing to pay a VIP ticket), spectators had to perform a series of tasks, or “actions”, such as volunteering, sending an email to a global leader, calling or making any other useful efforts to raise awareness of the fight against poverty.
But to what extent can the Global Citizen still achieve its goal in the remaining twelve years?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free concert really to persuade them to demand a call to action, or is it just another example of “clictivism” – a situation in which people really think they’re making a difference by signing a petition or sending a tweet?
Since 2011, Global Citizen has recorded more than 19 million “shares” on the part of its supporters, who advocate for many different goals.
According to the organization, these actions have helped encourage world leaders to make commitments and adopt policies amounting to more than US$37 billion that is expected to change the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
At the beginning of 2018, the group mentioned 390 commitments and announcements resulting from its actions, of which at least 10 billion dollars has already been disbursed or raised.
The group estimates that guaranteed funds have so far had a direct impact on nearly 649 million people around the world.
Among the main commitments is The Power of Nutrition, a U.S. partnership of investors and implementers who have pledged to “help children develop their full potential”, while promising Rwanda 35 million pounds Sterling assistance to end malnutrition in the country after receiving more than 4,700 messages from Global Citizens.
“Thanks to the support of the British government, donors, national governments and Global Citizens like you, we can make the social injustice of undernutrition a footnote in history,” Tracey Ullman, ambassador of The Power of Nutrition, told the crowd at a concert in London in April 2018.
The group also said that following more than 5 000 actions aimed at asking the UK to improve maternal and children’s nutrition, the government has announced funding for a project, The Power of Nutrition, which will benefit 5 million women and children through nutritional interventions.
In response to one of the most frequently asked questions on his website, “What makes you think it’s possible to end extreme poverty?”
Global Citizen said: “The way to get there will be long and difficult, sometimes we’ll fall and fail.
However, like the major civil rights and anti-apartheid movements before us, we’ll get there because we’re more powerful together.
Janet Jackson, The Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B, Janelle Monáe are among the artists who will perform this year in New York, where Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman will host the show.
The United States could call on the Navy to create a “blockade” to hamper Russian energy exports – Secretary of the Interior
In order to prevent Russian energy from entering the markets, including in the Middle East, Washington can “if necessary” appeal to the U.S. Navy, Ryan Zinke, the U.S. Secretary of the Interior, was quoted as saying by Washington Examiner.
Zinke said Russia’s presence in Syria – notably, where it operates at the request of the legitimate government – is a pretext that allows it to explore new energy markets.
“I believe they are in the Middle East with the aim of negotiating energy as they do in Eastern Europe, the southern centre of Europe,” he said.
According to the official representative, there are ways to remedy it.
“The United States, together with our Navy, can ensure that shipping lanes are open and, if necessary, obstruct to ensure that their energy is not put on the market,” he added.
Zinke was addressed to attendees held by the Consumer Energy Alliance, a non-profit organization that defines itself as the “spokesman for energy consumers” in the United States.
He compared Washington’s approaches to relations with Russia and Iran, stressing that they are actually the same.
“With regard to Iran and Russia, the economic option lies more or less in the exploitation and replacement of fuels,” he said, while referring to Russia saying that it has “more than one rope to its arc” with an economy dependent on fossil fuels.
These statements come at a time when the Trump administration has pledged to boost the export of its liquefied natural gas to Europe, replacing Russia, a much cheaper solution for European consumers.
To do this, representatives of the Trump administration, including U.S. President Donald Trump himself, are trying to convince Germany to get out of the “inappropriate” project of the Nord Stream 2 pipeline, which, according to Trump, makes the city of Berlin “prisoner” of Moscow.
On several occasions, Moscow pointed out that the US$11 billion Nord Stream 2 pipeline, which is expected to double the current capacity to reach 110 billion cubic meters, is a purely economic project.
The Kremlin argues that Washington’s staunch opposition to the project is simply driven by economic reasons and is an example of unfair competition.
In a September speech in Moscow following a meeting with Rick Perry, U.S. Secretary of Energy, Russian Energy Minister Alexander Novak said, “I believe we share this view that energy cannot be a tool of pressure and that consumers should be able to choose their suppliers.”
The position of the United States provoked negative reactions from Germany, which reaffirmed its involvement in this project.
Germany’s main industrial organization, the Federation of German Industries (BDI), called on the United States not to get involved in European energy policy or the bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
“I have a huge problem when a third country interferes with our energy supply,” said Dieter Kempf, director of the Federation of German Industries (BDI) following a recent meeting between German Chancellor Angela Merkel and Russian President Vladimir Putin.
Elizabeth Warren will seriously consider the possibility of running in the 2020 presidential election, says the Massachusetts senator
Massachusetts Senator Elizabeth Warren announced on Saturday that she would seriously consider the possibility of running in the presidential elections following the mid-term elections.
At a public meeting in Holyoke, Massachusetts, Warren confirmed that she would consider running.
“It’s time for women to go to Washington and repair our broken government, and for that, a woman will have to be at the top,” she says, according to The Hill.
“After November 6, I will seriously consider presenting my candidacy for the presidential elections.”
Warren said what she thought of President Donald Trump at the public rally, saying he was “leading this country in the wrong direction.”
“I’m very concerned about what Donald Trump is doing to our democracy,” she said.
Warren has been outspoken in his criticism of Trump and his Supreme Court candidate, Brett Kavanaugh.
On Friday, in a post on Twitter, Warren said, “Obviously we need an FBI investigation before we vote.”
However, a poll published Thursday showed that the majority of Warren’s voters do not believe they are expected to show up in 2020.
According to the University of Suffolk and Bostob Globe Policy Research Center poll, 58% of Massachusetts’ “probable” voters said that the senator should not show up.
32% were in favour of such a campaign.
The poll found that former Governor Deval Patrick was more supported, with 38% in favor of a potential campaign and 48% against it.
Other prominent Democrat names mentioned for a potential campaign in 2020 include former Vice President Joe Biden and Vermont Senator Bernie Sanders.
According to The Associated Press, Biden said he will make an official decision by January.
Sarah Palin Talks About Track Palin's PTSD During Donald Trump's Rally
Track Palin, 26, spent a year in Iraq after enlisting in September.
On Monday evening, he was arrested and charged in connection with an incident of domestic violence.
“What my own son currently experiences, what he feels when he comes home, I can connect him to other families who feel the consequences of PTSD and certain wounds with which our soldiers are coming back,” she told the public at the rally for Donald Trump in Tulsa, Oklahoma.
Palin called his arrest a “taboo subject” and said about his son and other veterans that “they come back a little different, hardened, wondering if we respect what their comrades and members of the air forces, and every other soldier, has given to the country.”
According to Dan Bennett, a spokesman for the Wasilla Police Service, he was arrested Monday in Wasilla, Alaska, and accused of assault and domestic violence against a woman, which interferes with a report of domestic violence and possession of an inebriated weapon.
18 states and the District of Columbia are in favor of a legal challenge to the new asylum policy
18 states and the District of Columbia are in favor of a legal challenge to the new U.S. policy that denies asylum to victims fleeing a gang or domestic violence.
On Friday in Washington, representatives of the 18 states and the district filed a brief in favor of an asylum seeker challenging the policy, NBC News said.
The plaintiff’s full name in Grace v. Sessions opened in August by the American Civil Liberties Union against federal policy has not been disclosed.
She said her partner “and sons, members of violent gangs,” had assaulted her, but U.S. authorities decided to reject her asylum application on July 20.
She's been detained in Texas.
State prosecutors who support Grace have described El Salvador, Honduras and Guatemala, which generate a large number of asylum applications in the United States, such as countries facing widespread problems of gangs and domestic violence.
The new U.S. asylum policy overturned a 2014 decision by the Immigrant Appeals Board, which allowed illegal immigrants fleeing domestic violence to seek asylum.
Karl Racine, the attorney general of the District of Columbia, said Friday in a statement that the new policy “ignores decades of state, federal and international law.”
“Federal law stipulates that all asylum applications must be examined on the basis of the particular facts and circumstances of the application, and such a prohibition violates this principle,” the amicus curiae said in his brief.
Lawyers have also argued in the brief that immigrant entry denial policy harms the U.S. economy, arguing that they are more likely to become entrepreneurs and “provide the necessary work.”
Attorney General Jeff Sessions ordered immigration judges in June to no longer grant asylum to victims fleeing spousal violence and gang violence.
“Asylum is granted to people who leave their country of origin because of persecution or fear on the grounds of race, creed, nationality, membership of a particular social group or political beliefs,” Sessions said on June 11 in its announcement.
“Asylum has never been designed to alleviate all problems – even the most serious – that people encounter every day around the world.”
Desperate rescue efforts in Palu while the number of deaths doubles in the race to find survivors
For the survivors, the situation is becoming more dramatic.
“I’m feeling very tense,” said Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, comforting her feverish little boy in Palu, in an evacuation center in the emptied city.
“At every moment, an ambulance brings back bodies.
“Clean water is rare.”
Residents were seen going into their destroyed homes, picking up soggy possessions and trying to save what they could find.
Hundreds of people have been injured and hospitals damaged by the 7.5 magnitude earthquake have been overwhelmed.
Some of the wounded, including Dwi Haris, who suffered a broken back and shoulders, rested in front of the military hospital in Palu, where patients were being treated outdoors due to persistent strong aftershocks.
Tears filled his eyes when he recounted how he felt the violent quake that shaken the hotel room on the fifth floor where he was staying with his wife and daughter.
“We didn’t have time to save ourselves.
“I’ve been crushed in the ruins of the wall, I believe,” Haris told Associated Press, adding that his family was in town for a wedding.
“I heard my wife calling for help, but after silence.
“I don’t know what happened to her or my child.
I hope they’re safe.”
U.S. ambassador accuses China of ‘bullying’ through ‘propaganda advertisements’
A week after a Chinese official newspaper passed a four-page advertisement touting the mutual benefits of trade between the U.S. and China in a U.S. daily, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of using the U.S. press to spread propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump on Wednesday referred to the surcharge paid by China Daily in the Des Moines Register — the best-selling newspaper in Iowa — after accusing China of trying to interfere in the Nov. 6 U.S. parliamentary elections, an accusation that China refutes.
By accusing Beijing of wanting to meddle in the U.S. elections, Trump marked what U.S. officials told Reuters to be a new phase in a growing Washington-led campaign to put pressure on China.
While it is common for foreign governments to place advertisements in order to promote trade, Beijing and Washington are currently paralyzed in a growing trade war that has led them to apply reciprocal tariffs to their imports.
At the beginning of the trade war, China’s tariff measures aimed to hit exporters from states like Iowa who supported Trump’s Republican Party, Chinese and American experts said.
Terry Branstad, U.S. ambassador to China and a long-time former governor of Iowa, a major exporter of agricultural products to China, said Beijing had harmed U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
China, Branstad writes in an opinion article published in Sunday’s Des Moines Register, “is doubling its intimidation by publishing propaganda in our own free press.”
“In disseminating its propaganda, the Chinese government is using the American tradition of freedom of expression and freedom of the press by inserting paid advertising into the Des Moines Register,” Branstad wrote.
“On the other hand, at the Beijing press window, you’ll find few protest voices and won’t see the true reflection of the differing opinions the Chinese can have about China’s troubling economic trajectory, because the media firmly obeys China’s Communist Party,” he wrote.
He added that “one of China’s most important newspapers avoided publishing” his article, without saying which newspaper.
Analysts say Republicans are alienating voters in the run-up to midterm elections due to Kavanaugh’s debacle
As many high-ranking Republicans defend Supreme Court-nominated Brett Kavanaugh in the context of sexual assault allegations, analysts have warned that a negative reaction, especially among women, could occur during the upcoming midterm elections.
The feelings surrounding this case have been extremely lively, and most Republicans have already publicly shown that they wanted the vote to take place.
In an article published Sept. 29 in The Hill, Grant Reeher, a professor of political science at Syracuse University’s Maxwell School, said “we can’t go back.”
Reeher added that he doubted that the last-minute efforts made by Senator Jeff Flake (R-Ariz.) in favor of an FBI investigation are enough to calm the anger of voters.
According to Karine Jean-Pierre, senior adviser and national spokesperson of the Progressive MoveOn group, “women will not forget what happened yesterday; they will not forget it tomorrow, and not in November,” she said on Sept. 28, according to the Washington newspaper.
Friday morning, Mic reports, the protesters sang “November’s month is coming!” in the Senate corridor as Republicans governing the Judiciary Committee have chosen to advance Kavanaugh’s appointment despite the testimony of Dr. Christine Blasey Ford.
“Democratic enthusiasm and motivation is going to be disproportionate,” said Stu Rothenberg, a non-partisan political analyst.
“People say it’s already high, that’s true.
But it could be even higher, especially among street voters and young voters between the ages of 18 and 29 who, even if they don’t like the president, often don’t vote.”
Even before Ford’s public deposition explains in detail the allegations of sexual assault against the Supreme Court nominee, analysts have hinted that the reaction would be likely to be heated if Republicans advanced confirmation.
According to NBC News, the former chairman of the Republican National Committee, Michael Steele, said earlier last week that “It has become a confusing mess for the GOP.”
“It’s not just about the committee’s vote or the final vote, it’s about Kavanaugh’s nomination, it’s also about how Republicans handled the situation and how they handled it,” Guy Cecil, director of U.S. Priorities, a group that helps elected Democrats, said.
As a result of testimonies from Ford and Kavanaugh, the Americans, however, seem divided about the credibility of both sides, a small majority leaning towards the latter.
A new YouGov survey found that 41% of those consulted absolutely or probably believed in Ford’s testimony, while 35% said they believed Kavanaugh absolutely or likely.
In addition, 38% claimed to believe that Kavanaugh probably or absolutely lied during his testimony, while only 30% said the same thing about Ford.
According to The Guardian, the FBI is investigating allegations made by Ford and at least another accuser, Deborah Ramirez, after pressure from Senator Flake.
Ford testified under oath last week before the Senate Judiciary Committee that she had been assaulted by Kavanaugh while intoxicated at the age of 17.
Ramirez claims that when they were studying at Yale in the 1980’s, the Supreme Court candidate allegedly showed him his genitals during a party.
The inventor of the World Wide Web plans to launch a New Internet to challenge Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, is launching an all-new company looking to compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The latest project of the technological legend, Inrupt, is a company that takes advantage of Berners-Lee’s Free Solid platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored as well as those authorized to access such information.
In an exclusive interview with Fast Company, Berners-Lee jokingly stated that Inrupt was aimed at “dominating the world.”
“We have to do it now,” he said of the launch.
“It’s a historical moment.”
The app uses Solid’s technology to enable people to create their own “online personal data store” or DPL.
It can contain contact lists, to-do lists, a calendar, a music library and other personal and professional tools.
It’s as if Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify were all available at the same time on a single browser.
The peculiarity of the online personal data store is that it is entirely up to the user to decide who can access what type of information.
The company calls this “personal responsibility via data”.
According to the company’s CEO, John Bruce, Inrupt’s idea is for the company to bring appropriate resources, processes and skills to make Solid accessible to everyone.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, a few developers contracting on the project and a community of voluntary coders.
Starting this week, technology developers around the world will be able to create their own decentralized applications using the tools available on Inrupt’s website.
Berners-Lee claimed that he and his team are not yet in discussions with “Facebook and Google” in order to know whether or not to make a complete change where all their business models are completely disrupted in an instant.
“We don’t ask them permission.”
In a Saturday article on Medium, Berners-Lee writes that [Inrupt’s] mission is to provide commercial energy and an ecosystem in order to protect the integrity and quality of the new web built on Solid.”
In 1994, at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet by creating the World Wide Web Consortium.
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the network’s neutrality debate.
Although he launched Inrupt, Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium, the Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
"I'm extremely optimistic about this new era of the web," Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: Celebration of an ecclesiastical of the First World War decorated with the Victoria Cross
The only ecclesiastical of the Church of England to have received the Victoria Cross during the First World War as a fighter was celebrated in his hometown 100 years later.
Lieutenant-Colonel Bernard Vann won this distinction on September 29, 1918, during the attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
He, however, was killed by a sniper four days later and did not know that he had obtained Britain’s highest military distinction.
His two grandsons unveiled a commemorative stone during a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire last Saturday.
Michael Vann, one of his grandsons, said it was “brilliantly symbolic” to unveil the stone exactly 100 years after his grandfather’s feat.
According to the London Gazatte, on 29 September 1918, Lieutenant-Colonel Vann steered his battalion on the Saint-Quentin Canal “through a very thick fog and under the violent firing of field cannons and machine guns.”
Later, he rushed to the firing line and, with the “greatest bravery,” guided the line before blowing up a field cannon on his own and knocking out three soldiers from the detachment.
On 4 October 1918, a German sniper killed Lieutenant-Colonel Vann, just over a month before the end of the war.
Michael Vann, 72, claimed that his grandfather’s actions were “a thing I’ll never live up to, but that makes me humble.”
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, also laided a wreath after the Brentwood Imperial Youth Band parade.
Michael Vann said he felt “very honored to be part of the parade” and added that “the courage of an authentic hero is reflected in the support that will be given to him by many people.”
Fans of mixed martial arts remained awakened all night to watch Bellator 206. Instead, they had Peppa Pig
Imagine that, you’ve been spending the whole night watching a busy Bellator 206, but you couldn’t see the main event.
In San José, the event had 13 fights, six of them on the main record, and was broadcast live throughout the night in the United Kingdom on Channel 5.
At 6 a.m
Some were dissatisfied to have stayed awake until the early hours of the morning especially for the fight.
A supporter on Twitter described the move to the children’s cartoon as “a kind of bad joke.”
Regarding transmission, Bellator’s Senior Vice President of Marketing and Communications, Dave Schwartz, said that “the government’s regulations state that at 6 a.m., this content wasn’t adapted, so they had to switch to children’s shows.”
""Peppa the Pig," yes."
Scott Coker, the president of Bellator Company, said they would work on their programming to include British viewers in the future.
“I believe that when I think about rebroadcasting, I think we can probably find a solution,” Coker said.
“However, it’s six o’clock in the morning one Sunday and we won’t be able to find a solution until Sunday our time, which is Monday their time.
But we're working on it.
Believe me, when the program switched, there were a lot of messages in both directions and they weren’t all friendly.
We were trying to fix it, we thought it was a technical problem.
But that wasn’t a problem, it was a government issue.
I can assure you this will not happen next time.
We’ll limit ourselves to five fights instead of six – as we usually do – and we’ve tried to give more for the fans and we’ve ruined everything.
It’s an unfortunate situation.”
Desert Island Discs : Tom Daley felt ‘inferior’ in terms of sexuality
Olympic diver Tom Daley claims to have grown up feeling inferior to others because of his sexuality – but it gave him the motivation to succeed.
The 24-year-old said he didn’t realize before going to high school that “not everyone [was] like me.”
As part of Lauren Laverne’s first Radio 4 Desert Island Discs show, he said he spoke about the issue of gay rights to give others “hope.”
He also said that becoming a parent made him less interested in winning the Olympics.
Kirsty Young, the show’s usual presenter, took several months of leave because of her illness.
Appeared as shipwrecked in Laverne’s first show, Daley confided that he felt “less than” all the other children growing up because “it wasn’t socially acceptable to love boys and girls.”
He said: “Even today, these feelings of being inferior and different are the real things that have given me the power and strength necessary for my success.”
In order not to disappoint everyone when they finally learned the truth about his sexuality, he wanted to prove that he was “something,” he continued.
The two-time Olympic bronze medalist is now a well-known LGBT activist and took advantage of his participation this year at the Commonwealth Games in Australia to ask more countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said that he expressed himself because he felt lucky to be able to live openly without ramifications and thus wanted to restore “hope” to others.
The three-time world champion claimed that he “didn’t expect” to fall in love with a man – American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, met in 2013
Daley last year marred the 20-year-old Oscar winner, but he said the age difference had never been a problem.
“When you’re experiencing so much at such a young age” – he competed in his first Olympics at the age of 14 and his father died of cancer three years later – he said it was difficult to find someone of the same age who experienced the same ups and downs.
In June, they became parents of a boy named Robert Ray Black-Daley, and Daley claimed that “his entire perspective” had changed.
“If you’d questioned me last year, I was just thinking about winning a gold medal,” he said.
“You know what, there are things that are more important than Olympic gold medals.
“My Olympic gold medal is Robbie.”
His son bears the same name as his father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 from brain cancer.
Daley confided that his father did not accept that he was going to die and that one of the last things he asked for was if they already had their tickets to London 2012, since he wanted to sit in the front row.
“I couldn’t say, ‘Dad, you won’t be there to be in the front row,” he said.
“I held him by the hand when he stopped breathing and he had to stop breathing and die for me to finally recognize that he wasn’t invincible,” he added.
The following year, Daley competed at the 2012 Olympics and won a bronze medal.
“That’s all I’ve dreamed of all my life – diving in front of a crowd at home at the Olympics, there’s nothing better,” he said.
In addition, it inspired him his first choice of song – Heather Small’s Proud – which had accompanied him in preparation for the Olympics and always gave him goosebump.
Desert Island Discs is on BBC Radio 4 on Sunday at 11:15 a.m., BST.
Mickelson out of shape on Ryder Cup bench on Saturday
The American Phil Mickelson will achieve a record this Sunday by playing his 47th Ryder Cup game, but he will have to straighten to prevent it from being an unfortunate stage.
Mickelson, who was competing in the biannual competition for the twelfth time, a record, was sidelined by captain Jim Furyk from Saturday’s fourballs and foursomes.
Instead of being at the heart of the action, as he has so often been for the United States, the fifteen great champion shared his day between the role of supporter and the refining of his game at the booth in order to remedy his ailments.
He has never been the greatest leader, even at the top of his career. At 48 years old, he is not the most suitable for the difficult Golf National, where the long rough systematically punishes misplaced blows.
And as if the course wasn’t dauntable enough in itself, Mickelson in Sunday’s ninth game won with British Open winner Francesco Molinari, who teamed up this week with Tommy Fleetwood to win their four games.
If the Americans, at four down at the beginning of the 12 singles games, make a good start, Mickelson’s game could prove to be absolutely crucial.
Furyk expressed confidence in his man, but he couldn’t say much else.
“He fully understood the role he had to play today, kicked me in the back, put his arm around me and said he’d be ready tomorrow,” Furyk said.
“He has great confidence in him.
He’s a legendary champion and he’s added so much to these teams in the past, just like this week.
I probably didn’t see him playing two games.
I expected more, but that’s how it went and that’s how we felt we had to proceed.
He wants to be there, like everyone else.”
Sunday, Mickelson will break Nick Faldo’s record for the most Ryder Cup games.
This could mark the end of a Ryder Cup career that has never fully reached the heights of its individual record.
Mickelson has 18 wins, 20 defeats and seven-half, although Furyk claimed that his presence brought concrete elements to the team.
He’s funny, sarcastic, spiritual, he likes to make fun of people, and he’s a great guy to have in the team room,” he explained.
“I think the younger players had fun competing against him this week, which was fun to see.
He’s doing much more than playing.”
European captain Thomas Bjorn knows the lead may soon disappear
Thomas Bjorn, the European captain, knows from experience that a significant advance before the singles of the last day of the Ryder Cup can easily turn into a painful course.
The Dane played his first game in 1997 in Valderrama, where a team whose captain was Seve Ballesteros was five points ahead of the Americans, but finally climbed to the top of the standings with the smallest possible gap on the finish line, winning 141⁄2-131⁄2.
“You’ren’t just remembering that we’re leading heavily in Valderrama; we’ve had a big lead in Brookline, where we’ve failed, as well as Valderrama, where we won, but narrowly,” said Bjorn, after observing the 2018 Ranking win 5-3 both on Friday and yesterday at the National Golf to lead 10-6.
History will prove to me and to all members of this team that this is not the end.
Go all over tomorrow.
Go ahead and do whatever it takes.
It’s not over until you have the points on the board.
We have one goal of trying to win this trophy, and that’s where we have to stay focused.
I’ve always said I’ve focused on the 12 players on our side, but we’re very aware of what’s happening on the other side – they’re the best players in the world.”
Delighted with the performances of his players on a difficult golf course, Bjorn added: “I would never advance on this point.
It’s a completely different pair of sleeves tomorrow.
Tomorrown, it is the individual performances that will be presented and it is quite different.
It’s fantastic to be with a teammate when things are going well, but when you’re there alone, you’re put to the test at your full capacity as a golfer.
That’s the message for the players, it’s to make the most of yourself tomorrow.
“When you leave your partner behind, he has to get the most out of himself, too.”
Unlike Bjorn, his counterpart Jim Furyk will seek to ensure that his players achieve better results individually than as teammates, with the exception of Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who scored three out of four.
Furyk himself experienced both ends of these setbacks of the last day, having been part of Brookline’s winning team before losing at the end of the “Miracle of Medinah”, where Europe snatched victory.
“I remember every word,” he explains in response to the question of how Ben Crenshaw, the captain in 1999, had done to rally his players on the last day.
“We’ve got 12 important meetings tomorrow, but you’d like to take that quick start as you’ve seen in Brookline, as you’ve seen in Medinah.
This dynamic, when it goes in one direction, puts a lot of pressure on the middle games.
“We’ve established our training accordingly and put the guys as we thought we had to, we’re trying to do magic tomorrow.”
Thomas was given the task of leading the counterattack and will face Rory McIlroy in the first-class duel, along with Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter, the other Europeans in the top half of the standings.
“I’ve accompanied this group of guys in this order because I think it covers the whole course,” Bjorn said of his selections in singles.
Germany's new warship further postponed
According to local media, the German Navy’s new frigate should have been put into service in 2014 to replace aging warships from the Cold War, but it won’t be in service until at least next year due to faulty systems and exponential spending.
According to the newspaper Die Zeit, which quotes a military spokesman, the commissioning of the “Rheinland-Pfalz”, the flagship of the all-new frigates of Baden-Württemberg, has been postponed until the first half of 2019.
The ship should have joined the Navy in 2014, but the worrying problems that occurred after delivery compromised the fate of this ambitious project.
The four Baden-Württemberg-class ships the Navy ordered in 2007 will replace the aging Bremen-class frigates.
It is certain that they will be equipped with a powerful gun, a range of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles as well as some stealth technologies, such as a reduced radar, infrared and acoustic signature.
Other important features include longer maintenance periods – it should be possible to deploy the most recent frigates for up to two years outside home ports.
According to Die Zeit, however, relentless delays mean that avant-garde war ships – which are expected to allow Germany to project its overseas force – will already be obsolete when they are put into service.
Last year, cursed frigate F125 made headlines when the German Navy officially refused to put the ship into service and sent it back to Blohm & Voss shipyard in Hamburg.
It was the first time the Navy had returned a ship to a shipbuilder after delivery.
The reasons for this return were little known, but the German media mentioned a number of “software and hardware flaws” that made the warship useless in the event of a deployment on a combat mission.
Software defects were particularly important because Baden-Württemberg-class ships will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors, which corresponds to half of the crews of the old Bremen-class frigates.
In addition, it appears that the vessel is significantly overloaded, which decreases its performance and reduces the Navy’s ability to make future improvements.
It is estimated that "Rheinland-Pfalz" of 7 000 tons weighs twice as heavily as similar-class ships used by Germans during World War II.
Beyond the failures of the computer system, the price of the project as a whole – including the training of the crew – also becomes a problem.
It is said to have reached the astronomical sum of 3.1 billion euros (3.6 billion dollars), against 2.2 billion initially.
The problems related to the new frigates are of particular importance in the light of the recent warnings that Germany’s naval force is diminishing.
Hans-Peter Bartels, head of the defense commission of the German parliament, acknowledged earlier this year that the Navy is in fact “short of ships able to deploy.”
The official stated that the situation had produced a snowball effect over time, as the old ships were decommissioned but no replacement vessels were designated.
He regretted that none of the Baden-Württemberg-class frigates had been able to join the Navy.
The National Trust oversees the secret life of bats
New research carried out in an area of the Scottish Highlands seeks to reveal how bats use the landscape in their quest for food.
It is hoped that the sightings will shed new light on the behaviour of these unique flying mammals and guide future conservation activities.
The study by researchers at the National Trust for Scotland will follow common and soprano pipistrels as well as long-eared brown bat and Daubenton at Inverewe Garden in Wester Ross.
Special check-in devices will be placed in key locations around the property to track bat activities throughout the season.
NHS staff and volunteers will also conduct mobile studies using portable detectors.
A professional sound analysis will determine the frequency of bat screams as well as the activities of the different species.
A habitat map and report will be developed to provide a detailed picture of their behaviour across the territory.
Rob Dewar, Nature Conservation Advisor for the NTS, hopes that the results will reveal the most important areas of habitat for bats and how they are used by each species.
This information will determine the benefits of habitat management work such as the creation of grasslands and how best to preserve forests for bats and other related species.
In Scotland and the United Kingdom, bat populations have declined significantly over the past century.
They are threatened by the construction and development work that affects the perches and results in the loss of their habitat.
Wind turbines and lighting can also pose a risk, as can papers and certain chemical treatments of building materials, as well as attacks from company cats.
Bats aren’t really blind.
However, given their nocturnal hunting habits, their ears are more useful than their eyes when it comes to capturing prey.
They use a sophisticated echolocation technique to spot insects and obstacles on their flight route.
Responsible for the protection of more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76 000 hectares of land in the country, the NTS takes bats very seriously.
He has ten trained experts, who regularly conduct polls, perch inspections and sometimes rescues.
The organisation has also established Scotland’s first and only bat reserve at the Threave site in Dumfries-et-Galloway, which is home to eight of Scotland’s ten species of bats.
David Thompson, manager of the field, says the site is the ideal territory for them.
“Here in Threave, we have an ideal place for bats,” he said.
“We’ve got old buildings, a lot of old trees and all the right habitat.
But there’s still a lot to learn about bats, so the work we’re doing here and in other places will help us better understand what bats need to thrive.”
It emphasizes the importance of verifying the presence of bats before carrying out the maintenance of the properties, because by unwittingly destroying a single maternity nest, we can cause the death of 400 females and young, or even the disappearance of an entire local population.
Bats are protected and it is illegal to kill them, disturb them, or destroy their perches.
Elisabeth Ferrell, the Scottish head of the Bat Conservation Trust, encouraged the public to help.
She said: “We still have a lot to learn about our bats and when it comes to many of our species, we don’t know what’s going on in their populations right now.”
Ronaldo refutes rape charges as his lawyers prepare to sue a German magazine
Cristiano Ronaldo called the rape charges against him “false news,” and said people “want to make themselves known” using his name.
His lawyers are about to sue the German magazine Der Spiegel, which published the allegations.
The attacker from Portugal and Juventus has been accused of raping an American named Kathryn Mayorga in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
He then paid her 375 000 dollars to remain silent about the incident, Der Spiegel said last Friday.
Addressing its 142 million fans in a live video posted on Instagram, hours after the allegations, Ronaldo, 33, called the information “false news.”
“No, no, no, no, not.
What they said today is false information,” the five-time Ballon d’Or laureate told the camera.
“They want to advertise themselves using my name.
It's normal.
They want to be famous by saying my name, but that's part of the job.
“I’m a happy man and everything’s fine,” he added with a smile.
According to Reuters, Ronaldo’s lawyers are preparing to prosecute Der Spiegel over these allegations, which they described as “unacceptable denunciation of suspicions in the field of privacy.”
Lawyer Christian Schertz said the player would seek compensation for “moral damages of an amount proportional to the severity of the offence, which is probably one of the most serious violations of human rights in recent years.”
The alleged incident took place in June 2009 in a sequel to the hotel-casino le Palms in Las Vegas.
After meeting at a nightclub, Ronaldo and Mayorga apparently returned to the player’s room, where an anal rape allegedly occurred, according to documents filed with the Clark County court in Nevada.
Mayorga claims that Ronaldo got on his knees after the presumed incident and assured him he was a “well guy” at “99%” but that he didn’t know what was going on with the remaining “1%.”
The documents allege that Ronaldo confirmed that the couple had had sex, but that they were consensual.
Mayorga also claimed to have surrendered to the police and that photos of her wounds had been taken in a hospital, but that she had subsequently accepted an amicable settlement because she felt “terrified by reprisals” and feared “public humiliation.”
The 34-year-old woman says she’s now seeking to overturn the settlement because she remains traumatized by the alleged incident.
Ronaldo was about to join Real Madrid by leaving Manchester United at the time of this alleged aggression, and this summer he joined the Italian giant Juve as part of a contract of 100 million euros.
Brexit : The United Kingdom "would regret forever" the departure of car manufacturers.
According to Greg Clark, the Secretary of State for Trade, the United Kingdom “would regret it forever” if it were to lose its status as the world’s leader in car manufacturing after Brexit.
He added that it was “worrying” that Toyota UK had told the BBC that it would temporarily stop production at its factory in Burnaston, near Derby, if Britain withdrew from the EU without a deal.
“We need a deal,” Clark said.
Japan’s automaker said the impact of delays at the border in the event of a no-deal Brexit could cost jobs.
The Burnaston plant – which manufactures Toyota’s Auris and Avensis vehicles – built nearly 150 000 cars last year, 90% of which were exported to other countries in the European Union.
“I consider that if Britain leaves the EU at the end of March, production will stop in our factory,” said Marvin Cooke, Toyota’s director in Burnaston.
Other British automakers, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover, have voiced their fear of leaving the EU without an agreement on the operation of cross-border trade.
For example, BMW indicates that it will close its Mini plant in Oxford for a month following Brexit.
The biggest concerns are what automakers call risks to the supply chain in the event of no-deal Brexit.
Toyota’s production line operates in “tense flow”, parts arrive every 37 minutes from suppliers in the UK and the EU for vehicles made to order.
If on March 29, the United Kingdom leaves the EU without a deal, there could be disturbances at the border which, according to the industry, could lead to delays and coin shortages.
It would be impossible for Toyota to hold the inventory more than a day at its factory in Derbyshire, the company said, and therefore production would be stopped.
According to Clark, Theresa May’s Chequers plan for future relations with the EU is “precisely calibrated in order to avoid these border controls.”
“We need a deal. We want to have the best deal that will, as I said, not only take advantage of the current success, but also seize this opportunity,” he said in the BBC’s Radio 4’s Today.
“The evidence, not only of Toyota but of other manufacturers, is that we absolutely have to be able to maintain what turned out to be a great set of supply chains.”
While Toyota was unable to specify how long production would be shut down, it warned that in the longer term, the additional costs would reduce the plant’s competitiveness and end up costing jobs.
Peter Tsouvallaris, union coordinator Unite at the factory who has been working in Burnaston for 24 years, said his members were increasingly worried: “In my experience, once these jobs disappear, they never come back.”
A government spokesman said: “We have proposed a clear and credible plan for our future relations with the EU.”
White House Announces Trump and Rosenstein Meeting May Be Delayed
According to the White House’s announcement on Sunday, the meeting, which has significant stakes, between Donald Trump and Deputy Attorney-General Rod Rosenstein could be “deflected by a week,” while the struggle for Supreme Court candidate Brett Kavanaugh continues.
Rosenstein oversees the work of Robert Mueller, the special adviser investigating Russian interference in the elections, the links between Trump’s aides and Russia, and the president’s possible obstructions to justice.
The question of whether or not Trump will dismiss the deputy attorney general, and thus endanger Mueller’s independence, has been fueling rumors in Washington for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein had talked about wearing a microphone in order to record conversations with Trump and the possibility of eliminating the president by the 25th amendment.
Rosenstein denied these claims.
However, last Monday, he showed up at the White House when he was about to resign.
Instead, a meeting with Trump, which was then at the United Nations in New York, was announced for Thursday.
Trump said he “would not prefer not” to dismiss Mr. Rosenstein, but the meeting was postponed in order to avoid a clash with the Senate Judiciary’s hearing before Kavanaugh and Dr Christine Blasey Ford, one of the women who accused him of sexual assault.
On Friday, Trump asked the FBI to conduct a week-long investigation into complaints against Kavanaugh, further delaying the Senate vote.
On Sunday, Trump’s press secretary, Sarah Sanders, made an appearance on Fox News.
Asked about the meeting with Rosenstein, she said: “No date has been set for that, it could be this week, I could see if we’re pushing back for another week given all the other things happening at the Supreme Court.
But we’ll see and I like to keep the press informed.”
The claim would be disputed by some reporters: Since Sept. 10, Sanders hasn’t held any press conferences at the White House.
Anistator Chris Wallace asked why.
Sanders replied that the scarcitity of the lectures wasn’t due to an aversion to “demagogical” television reporters, although she said, “I have nothing against the fact that they’re impressing the gallery.”
Then she hinted that direct contact between Trump and the press will increase.
Without citing evidence, she added that “the president participates in more question-and-answer sessions than any other president before him”: “We checked the numbers.”
Press conferences will always take place, Sanders said, “if the press has the chance to directly question the President of the United States, it’s infinitely better than talking with me.
We’re trying to do it frequently, you’ve seen it well in recent weeks, and it’s going to take the place of a press conference where you can talk to the President of the United States.”
On a regular basis, Trump responds to questions when he leaves the White House or when he participates in public sessions or press conferences with dignitaries.
It’s rare for him to give solo press conferences there.
This week in New York, the president may have demonstrated why, by making a casual appearance and at times a bizarre appearance in front of the gathered journalists.
Regarding fears about Brexit, the Secretary of Health writes to EU workers at NHS Scotland
The Secretary of Health has written to EU officials working within the Scottish NHS to express the country’s gratitude to them and wish them to remain in office after Brexit.
Scottish lawmaker Jeane Freeman sent a letter less than six months before the UK withdrew from the EU.
The Scottish Government has already pledged to cover the cost of applications for European citizens working in its devolved public services.
Freeman wrote in his letter: “During the summer, negotiations on the withdrawal continued between the UK and the EU and are expected to lead to decisions this autumn.
However, the British government has also stepped up its preparations in the event of a lack of agreement.
I understand that this period must be very troubling for all of you.
That’s why I wanted to reiterate how I value the contribution of each staff member, regardless of nationality.
Colleagues from across the EU and beyond bring valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of health services, and benefit the patients and communities we serve.
Scotland is quite your home and we really want you to stay here.”
Following a head injury, Christion Abercrombie undergoes emergency surgery
According to Mike Organ of the Tennessee State, Christion Abercrombie, the linebacker of the Tennessee Tigers, had emergency surgery after a head injury on Saturday in the defeat of the Vanderbilt Commodores 31-27.
Rod Reed, Tennessee’s chief state coach, told reporters that the injury occurred just before halftime.
“He came on the sidelines and collapsed there,” Reed said.
Trainers and medical staff administered oxygen to Abercrombie on the sideline before placing it on a stretcher and taking it for another examination.
A representative from the state of Tennessee told Chris Harris of WSMV in Nashville, Tennessee, that Abercrombie was out of the operating room at the Vanderbilt Medical Center.
Harris said there was “no details yet about the type or extent of the injury” and that the state of Tennessee was trying to determine when the injury occurred.
Abercrombie, a second-year student athlete, is in his first season with the state of Tennessee after his transfer from Illinois.
He got five tackles in total on Saturday before leaving the game, bringing his total of the season to 18 tackles.
Foreign buyers will be subject to a higher stamp duty if they buy property in the UK
Foreign buyers will have to pay higher stamp duties if they buy property in the UK and the extra money will be used to help the homeless under the new conservative plans
This measure will negate the success of Corbyn’s campaign to attract young voters
The increase in stamp duty will be imposed on those who do not pay taxes in the United Kingdom
The Treasury expects to collect up to 120 million Sterling pounds a year to help the homeless.
Foreign buyers will be subject to a higher stamp duty tariff if they buy property in the UK – the extra money will be used to help the homeless, Theresa May announced today.
This measure will be seen as an initiative to neutralize the success of Jeremy Corbyn’s campaign to attract young voters by committing to providing more affordable housing and targeting high-income earners.
The increase in stamp duty will be imposed on individuals and businesses that do not pay taxes in the UK, and the additional money will be used to strengthen the government’s efforts in the fight to help the homeless.
This surcharge – in addition to the current stamp duty, including the higher levels introduced two years ago on second homes and purchases-leases – could reach 3%.
The Treasury expects that the project will collect up to 120 million Sterling pounds a year.
Approximately 13% of London’s new buildings are bought by non-residents of the United Kingdom, which drives up prices and prevents first-time buyers from taking a place in the housing market.
Many of the country’s rich areas – particularly in the capital – have become “ghost cities” because of the high number of foreign buyers who mostly stay outside the country.
This new policy comes just weeks after Boris Johnson demanded a reduction in stamp duty in order to help more young people acquire their first home.
Accusing major construction companies of keeping real estate prices at a high level by grabbing land without using it, he urged Ms. May to give up allowances on affordable housing in order to address the “real estate disgrace” in the UK.
Mr Corbyn presented an attractive series of reform proposals in the housing sector, including rent control and the removal of “faulty” evictions.
He also wished to give the councils broader powers to build new dwellings.
May said: “Last year, I said that I would dedicate my position as Prime Minister to the rebirth of the British dream – that every new generation would have a better life.
To do so, we need to repair our broken housing market.
Great Britain will always be open to anyone who wants to live, work and build life here.
It is unfair, however, that it is as easy for individuals who do not reside in the UK, as well as for foreign companies, to buy a home as it is for hard-working British residents.
For far too many people, the dream of accessing the property has become remote and the misery of the homeless remains too real.”
Jack Ross: My final ambition is to lead Scotland.
Sunderland technician Jack Ross explains that his “ultimate ambition” is to become the Scottish coach at some point.
The 42-year-old Scot takes pleasure in taking on the challenge of reviving the North-Eastern club, which currently occupies the third place in Ligue 1, three points behind the leader.
He joined Stadium of Light this summer after taking St Mirren to the Scottish Premier League last season.
“I wanted to play for my country as a player.
I’ve been selected for Division B and that’s it,” Ross told BBC Scotland Sportsound.
“But I grew up following Scotland’s matches in Hampden with my father and that’s something I’ve never forgotten.
This opportunity would, on the other hand, only happen if I succeeded in the management of the club.”
Ross’ predecessors as Sunderland coaches are Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O’Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Alloa Athletic’s former technician says he didn’t hesitate to stand behind such iconic names in such a big club, having already declined Barnsley and Ipswich Town’s proposals.
“For me, the success of the moment will be measured in the light of the question ‘can I bring this club back to the Premier League?’
Due to the structure and infrastructure of this club, it undoubtedly belongs to the Premier League,” he added.
“It’s not easy to get there, but I’ll probably only see myself as a success here if I manage to get the club back there.”
Ross has only a three-year career as a coach, after a period as an assistant at Dumbarton and 15 months with the Hearts coaches.
He then helped Alloa recover from a relegation in the third division and transformed St Mirren, who was on the verge of relegation, into a great winner of the championship the following season.
Ross says he’s more comfortable now than during his career in Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academic.
Alloa’s care “was probably a real crossroads,” he recalls.
“I really thought that being a coach suited me better than playing.
It sounds strange because I did well, that I was making a good living and enjoyed some appreciable peaks.
But playing is sometimes difficult.
There is a lot of detail to settle every week.
I continue to experience this in terms of stress and tension at work, but being a trainer suits me very well.
I’ve always wanted to lead and now I’m doing it, I’m more comfortable in my skin than I’ve been in my entire adult life.”
Find the full interview on Sportsound on Sunday, September 30 on Radio Scotland between 12:00 p.m. and 1:00 p.m
According to a survey, the ideal time for a pint is 5:30 p.m. on Saturdays.
The heatwave of the summer increased the revenues of struggling British pubs, but increased the pressure on restaurant chains.
Figures indicate that groups of bars and pubs recorded a 2.7% increase in sales in July, but revenues in restaurants fell by 4.8.
CGA’s consulting firm Peter Martin, who compiled the data, said: “The permanent sunshine and England’s longer-than-expected participation in the World Cup caused July to follow June’s model, where bars rose by 2.8%, except for restaurants, which suffered an even greater shock.
The 1.8% decline recorded in June in the catering sector only worsened in July.
The pubs and bars where alcoholic beverages are served are by far the most fashionable, with more points of resemblance than declining restaurants.
Food pubs have also suffered from the effects of the sun, but not as much as restaurateurs.
It seems that people just want to go out for a drink.
In pubs and bars, beverage sales increased by 6.6% over the month and food decreased by 3%.”
Paul Newman, an analyst specializing in leisure and hospitality RSM, said: “These results follow the trend observed since the end of April.
The weather and the impact of major social or sporting events remain the most important factors when it comes to sales in the out-of-home market.
It’s not surprising that restaurant groups continue to struggle, although a 4.8% year-on-average sales decline is particularly painful, in addition to ongoing cost pressures.
The long and hot summer could not have been less opportune for food business operators and the weather will tell us if the more moderate temperatures we experienced in August will give a well-deserved respite.”
The total increase in sales in pubs and restaurants, including new openings, was 2.7% in July, reflecting the slowdown in implementation.
Industry sales are monitored by the Coffer Peach Tracker for the U.K.’s pubs, bar and restaurant sector, which collects and analyzes performance data from 47 operating groups, with a combined turnover of more than 9 billion pounds Sterling and is the standard in the established industry.
On social media, one in five children has secret accounts he hides from his parents
According to a survey, one in five children – a few of whom are only 11 years old – has secret social media accounts that he hides from his parents and teachers
A survey of 20 000 high school students revealed an increase in “false Insta” pages
The news heightened concerns about the publication of sexual content
20% of students reported having a “primary” account to show their parents
One in five children – some of whom are only 11 years old – open an account on social media that it keeps hidden from adults.
A survey of 20,000 high school students revealed a rapid growth of “fake Insta accounts” – a reference to the Instagram photo-sharing site.
The news heightened concerns about the publication of sexual content.
20% of students reported managing a cleansed “primary” account to show it to their parents, while having a private account.
A mother who fell by chance at her 13-year-old daughter’s secret site uncovered a teenager who urged others to “rape me.”
According to the study conducted by Digital Awareness UK and the Headmasters and Headmistresses Conference (HMC) of independent schools, 40% of young people aged 11 to 18 have a dual profile, with half of pupils recognising having private accounts.
CSM Chief Mike Buchanan said: “It’s worrying that so many teenagers are tempted to create places online where parents and teachers don’t find them.”
Eilidh Doyle will represent the athletes on the Scottish Athletics Board of Directors.
Eilidh Doyle was elected to the Scottish Athletics Board of Directors as non-executive director at the annual general meeting of the governing body.
Doyle is Scotland’s most athletics athlete and President Ian Beattie has described this choice as a great opportunity for the sport’s leaders to build on his extensive international experience these ten years.
“Eilidh enjoys immense respect in the Scottish, British and global athletics community, and we are confident that athletics in Scotland would benefit enormously from their participation in the council,” said Beattie.
Doyle said: “I really want to be the voice of the athletes and I want to be able to really make my contribution and guide this sport in Scotland.”
The American, who won 200-meter and 400-metre events in 1996 at the Atlanta Olympics where he won four gold medals and is now a regular BBC commentator, has been unable to walk after suffering a transitory ischemic attack.
He wrote on Twitter: “A month ago I had a stroke.
I couldn't walk anymore.
Doctors claimed that only the weather would say if I recovered or how much.
It was a trying job, but I recovered completely, I learned to walk again and today I do agility exercises!
“Thank you for the messages of encouragement!”
Online, an advertisement on the milk that compares mothers and cows divides opinions
An online advertisement from a company offering a breast pump compares nursing mothers to cows during milking.
In order to mark the launch of what is called the “first silent breast pump in the world”, Elvie, a consumer tech company, has launched a daring publicity inspired by a music video to show the freedom that her new breast pump offers breastfeeding women.
In a barn of hay-filled cows, four real mothers dance to the rhythm of a song whose lyrics are: “Yes, I’m leaving myself, but you don’t see a dick” and “In case you didn’t notice it, these are my breasts, not breasts.”
The chorus continues: “Pump it, pump it, I feed the babies, pump it, pump it, pump it, I help the women dirting the milk.”
However, the advertisement, which was posted on the company’s Facebook page, sparked controversy online.
With 77 000 visualizations and hundreds of comments, the video has received contradictory reactions from the public, many of whom say it sheds light on the “horrors” of the dairy industry.
“A poor decision to use cows to promote this product.
Like us, they have to get pregnant and give birth to produce milk, except that their babies are abducted to them in the days following their birth.”
Elvie’s breast pump discreetly adapts into a breastfeeding bra (Elvie/Mother)
Another made comments: “It’s an understandable trauma for both the mother and the baby.
“But yeah, why don’t you use them to advertise a breast pump for mothers who keep their babies?”
Someone else added: “An advertisement so offline.”
Others defended the advertisement, for example a woman admitting that she found the song “hilaring”.
“I think it’s an idea of genius.
I’d have bought one if I was still breastfeeding.
Pumper made me feel like a cow.
Advertising is a little crazy, but I took it for what it was.
It’s a great product,” someone wrote.
Someone else commented: “This is a fun commercial for moms who pull their milk (often at their workplace or in the bathroom) and feel like ‘cows’.
It’s not an advertisement that praises the dairy industry.”
At the end of the video, the women reveal that they have danced with the discreet breast pumps concealed in their bra.
The concept behind the campaign is based on the idea that many women who use the milktail say they have the impression of being cows.
However, Elvie’s breast pump is completely silent, wireless or pipes, and discreetly fits into a nursing bra, which gives women the freedom to move, hold their baby and even get out while pulling their milk.
Ana Balarin, partner and DPE at Mother commented: “Elvie breast pump is such a revolutionary product that it deserves a bold and provocative launch.
By drawing a parallel between breastfeeding mothers and dairy cows, we wanted to focus on extracting breast milk and all its challenges, while expressing in a funny and reliable way the incredible sense of freedom that this new milt will provide.
This isn’t the first time Elvie’s milkcruff has made headlines.
During Fashion Week in London, a mother of two appeared on designer Marta Jakubowski’s podium while she was using this product.
Hundreds of children of migrants have silently settled at a camp on the Texas border
The number of detained migrant children has risen sharply although the number of monthly border crossings has remained relatively stable. This is partly due to the fact that the Trump administration’s harsh speeches and policies made it possible to complicate children’s placements among respondents.
Traditionally, most respondents themselves were undocumented immigrants and they feared compromising their own ability to stay in the country by coming to a child’s rescue.
Risk increased in June, when federal authorities announced that potential respondents and adult members of their households would have to submit their fingerprints and that this data would be forwarded to immigration authorities.
Matthew Albence, a senior official in the Immigration and Customs Service, testified last week before Congress that the agency had arrested dozens of people who had applied for sponsorship of unaccompanied minors.
The agency subsequently confirmed that 70% of those arrested had no criminal record.
“Nearly 80% of the people who are referees or members of their families are here illegally, and many of them are foreign criminals.
Therefore, we continue to pursue these people,” said Mr. Albence.
According to shelter workers, in order to deal more quickly with the cases of children, the authorities have introduced new rules that will require some of them to appear in court in the month following their detention, rather than after 60 days, which was the norm before.
Many of them will appear via videoconferencing, rather than in person, to plead their case with respect to their legal status before an immigration judge.
Those deemed ineligible will soon be deported.
According to stakeholders and the reports that have been presented in recent months by the network of shelters, the longer children remain in detention, the more likely they are to become distressed or depressed, which can lead to outbreaks of violence or attempts to flee.
In an institution that is large like Tornillo, these concerns are exacerbated by the fact that signs that a child is in difficulty are more likely to be overlooked due to their height, according to child rights advocates.
They added that moving children to the camp without giving them the time to prepare emotionally or say goodbye to their friends could accentuate a trauma that many struggle to deal with.
Syria is calling on the US, French and Turkish “occupying forces” to withdraw immediately.
In an address to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem also called on Syrian refugees to return home, even though the war in that country is now in its eighth year.
Moualem, who is also Deputy Prime Minister, said that foreign forces were on Syrian soil illegally, under the pretext of fighting terrorism, and that they would “be treated accordingly.”
“They have to withdraw immediately and unconditionally,” he told the assembly.
Moualem argued that the “war on terror is almost over” in Syria, while more than 360 000 people have died since 2011 and millions more have been uprooted from their homes.
He said that Damascus would continue “to fight this sacred battle until all Syrian territories are purged” from both terrorist groups and from any “illegal foreign presence.”
The United States has some 2 000 troops in Syria, most of whom train and advise both Kurdish and Syrian Arab forces opposing President Bashar al-Assad.
In this country ravaged by the war, France has more than 1 000 soldiers there.
Moualem said about the refugees, that the conditions were conducive to their return, and accused “some Western countries” of “propagating irrational concerns” that deterred refugees from returning.
“We have called on the international community and humanitarian organizations to ensure that these returns are facilitated,” he said.
“They are politicizing what should be a purely humanitarian issue.”
The United States and the European Union have indicated that there will be no support for the reconstruction of Syria until a political agreement between Assad and the opposition to end this war is reached.
UN diplomats say that a recent agreement between Russia and Turkey for the creation of a buffer zone in Idlib’s latest rebel fortress has made it possible to advance political negotiations.
The Russian-Turkish deal avoided a large-scale assault on the province where three million people live, by Russian-backed Syrian forces.
Moualem stressed, however, that the agreement provided for “precise deadlines” and expressed hope that military measures will target jihadists, in particular Nusra Front fighters, who are linked to Al-Qaeda and who “will be eliminated.”
Staffan de Mistura, representative of the United Nations, hopes soon to convene the first meetings of a new committee bringing together members of the government and the opposition in order to draft a post-war constitution and prepare the way for elections in Syria.
Moualem defined the conditions for the Syrian government’s participation in the committee, stating that the group’s work should be limited “to the revision of articles of the current constitution,” and made a warning against interference.
Why Will Trump Win a Second Term
According to this logic, Mr. Trump would be re-elected in 2020, unless, as many liberal viewers probably hope, impeachment and scandal lead him to prematurely leave his presidency.
Which would probably be “The most spectacular finale of a presidency!”
At the moment, the public does not show any signs of fatigue.
Since 2014, peak-hour odds have more than doubled, reaching 1.05 million on CNN, and have nearly tripled to 1.6 million on MSNBC.
According to Nielsen, Fox News has an average of 2.4 million viewers in prime time, up from 1.7 million four years ago, and MSNBC’s Rachel Maddow Show, which reaches 3.5 million viewers at major nights, is the first cable TV channel.
“It’s a case that attracts people because it’s not about something that we understood,” said Neal Baer, anchor of the ABC drama series “Designated Survivor,” which speaks of a minister who becomes president after an attack destroys the Capitol.
Nell Scovell, a seasoned comedian and author of “Just the Funny Parts: “And some irrefutable truths about slipping into the Hollywood Boys’ Club,” has another theory.
She recalls taking a taxi in Boston prior to the 2016 election.
The driver told him he was going to vote for Mr. Trump.
Why, she asked.
“He replied, ‘Because he makes me laugh,’” Scovell told me.
Chaos has playworthy value.
Of course, contrary to everything that’s happening on television, Washington’s scripts could determine the future of the Roe v. Wade case, if immigrant families can come together and the health of the global economy.
Disconnecting is a luxury that only the wealthiest viewers can afford.
And however, it goes beyond being an informed citizen when we find ourselves watching for six hours a panel of experts debating how Bob Woodward used “deep know-how” for his book “Fear,” on Paul Manafort’s 15,000-dollar austrich leather veston (“a garment full of pride” according to the Washington Post) and the consequences of the extravagant descriptions
I’ll never see Super Mario the same.
“If some of what he’s doing gives the impression of seeing a reality TV show, it’s because he’s making you swallow something every night,” said Brent Montgomery, managing director of Wheelhouse Entertainment and creator of “Pawn Stars,” about Trump’s changing teams and daily turnaround (fighting with the NFL.
We can't afford to miss an episode otherwise we're lagging behind.
When I contacted Mr. Fleiss this week, it was 26 degrees at home on the north shore of Kauai, but he washed inside to watch MSNBC while recording CNN.
He couldn’t detach himself, not with Brett Kavanaugh preparing to confront the Senate Judiciary Committee and the future of the Supreme Court at stake.
“I remember the time when we animated all these crazy broadcasts and people said: ‘This is the beginning of the end of Western civilization,’” Fleiss told me.
“I thought it was kind of a joke, but it turns out they were right.”
Amy Chozick, a General Journalist of the Times covering business, politics and the media, is the author of the memoir “Chasing Hillary.”
External money invades the tightest mid-term elections
It’s not surprising that the 17th district of Pennsylvania is flooded with money, thanks to a readjustment of congressional constituencies that allowed two incumbents to compete for the same seat.
This recently restructured Pittsburgh-suburban district is the seat of Democratic Representative Conor Lamb, who landed his seat in another constituency in the special elections last spring.
Lamb competes with another incumbent, Republican Keith Rothfus, who currently represents the former 12th district of Pennsylvania, which straddles the new 17th district.
The cards were redesigned when Pennsylvania’s Supreme Court ruled in January that the former boroughs were unconstitutionally manipulated in favor of the Republicans.
The race for the new 17th district kicked off a clash regarding the financing of election campaigns between the major branches of party funding, the Congressional Committee for the Democratic Campaign (CCCD) and the Republican National Campaign Committee (CNCR).
Lamb became a well-known name in Pennsylvania after a narrow victory in March at a very popular special election in the 18th District of the Pennsylvania Congression.
This seat had been owned by a Republican for more than a decade and President Donald Trump won in the district by 20 points.
Politologists have given the Democrats a slight advantage.
The United States considered penalizing El Salvador for its support for China and then retreated
The diplomats found that the Dominican Republic and Panama had already recognized Beijing, which Washington did not oppose.
“Trump had a friendly meeting with Panamanian President Juan Carlos Varela in June 2017 and owned a hotel in Panama until the partners expel the leadership of the Trump Organization.
Representatives of the State Department have decided to summon the heads of U.S. diplomatic missions in El Salvador, the Dominican Republic and Panama to protest “their recent decisions to no longer recognize Taiwan,” Heather Nauert, the department’s spokesman, said earlier this month in a statement.
But sanctions have only been planned against El Salvador, which benefited from an estimated U.S. aid of 140 million dollars in 2017, in particular to control narcotics and provide it with development and economic support.
The envisaged sanctions, which included reductions in financial aid and visa-targeted restrictions, would have been painful for the Central American country and its high rates of unemployment and homicide.
As internal meetings continued, representatives from North and Central America delayed a high-level conferencing that focused on security and economic prosperity to follow up on a similar meeting that took place last year, seen as a breakthrough in efforts to prevent immigrants from travelling to the United States.
But in mid-September, senior administration officials made it clear that they wanted the conference to continue, putting an end to any consideration of sanctions against El Salvador.
Vice-President Mike Pence is now speaking at the conference, scheduled for mid-October, to show the importance that the administration attaches to the meeting, the diplomats said.
And the three U.S. emissaries quietly returned to El Salvador, Panama and the Dominican Republic, with no new messages or harsh punishments from Washington.
Bolton’s White House spokesman declined to comment on details of the debate that have been described by three U.S. dignitaries, including two diplomats, who agreed to discuss internal deliberations on condition of anonymity.
Their remarks were corroborated by an independent analyst close to the administration, who also remained anonymous.
Studying History
The next tile could be special counsel Robert Mueller’s report on Trump’s possible impediment to justice. The public record is now backed by substantial evidence.
Mueller would also direct his investigation into a collusiveness of Mr. Trump’s campaign with Russia in his attack on our elections.
If congressional control changes hands, Mr. Trump will find himself held accountable to this body, as he prepares to represent himself in front of voters and, perhaps, before a jury composed of his peers.
These are many “yes”, and I do not claim that Mr. Trump’s fall is inevitable – or that of his counterparts in Europe.
We all have decisions to make on both sides of the Atlantic that will have an impact on the potential duration of this struggle.
In 1938, the German officers were about to organize a coup against Hitler, if only the West had stood up to him and repelled the Czechoslovaks in Munich.
We failed and missed an opportunity to avoid the years of carnage that followed.
The course of history revolves around these cleavages and the inevitable march of democracy accelerates or is delayed.
Americans are currently facing a number of these cleavages.
What will we do if Mr. Trump dismisses Deputy Attorney General Rod Rosenstein, the man who controls Mr. Mueller’s investigation?
Rosenstein has been in a difficult situation since the newspaper reported that, last year, he offered to secretly register the president and made hypotheses about his inability to perform his duties.
Mr. Rosenstein states that the Times report is inaccurate.
“What will we answer if the FBI’s recent investigation into Brett Kavanaugh is not exhaustive or impartial – or if he is confimed to the Supreme Court despite sexual assault charges and dishonest testimonies?
And above all, are we going to vote in the mid-term elections for a Congress that will hold Mr. Trump accountable?
If we don't pass these ordeals, democracy will pass a long winter.
But I am convinced that we will not fail because of the lesson I have learned in Prague.
My mother was a Czechoslovak Jew deported to Auschwitz under the Nazi regime, which in the past occupied my residence as an ambassador.
She survived, immigrated to America, and, sixty years later, sent me to light Sabbath candles on the swastika table.
With this heritage as a legacy, I can’t not be optimistic about our future.”
Norman Eisen, a senior partner at the Brookings Institution, is president of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington and the author of The Last Palace: Europe’s Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House.
Graham Dorrans of the Rangers, confident against Rapid Vienna
The Rangers will receive the Rapid Vienna on Thursday, while knowing that the victory against the Austrians, following the impressive draw in Spain against Villarreal at the beginning of the month, will place them in a solid position to qualify from Group G in the Europa League.
Knee injury deprived midfielder Graham Dorrans from his first appearance of the season until the 2:2 draw against Villarreal, but he is convinced the Rangers are able to derive the best from such a result to achieve feats.
“It was a positive point for us because Villarreal is a quality team,” said the 31-year-old forward.
“We got into the game thinking about getting something and we left with one point.
We may have won at the end, but, overall, a draw was probably a fair result.
They were probably better in the first half and we came back in the second half and we were the best team.
On Thursday, it’s another important European evening.
With a little luck, we can expect to get three points, but it will be a difficult game because they made a good result in their last game, yet I’m sure we can continue and get a positive result with the crowd behind us.
Last year has been really difficult, between my injuries and the changes made to the club as such, but there’s a satisfaction factor about this place right now.
The team is good and the guys have a lot of fun; the training is quality.
Let’s hope to move forward now, leave last season behind us and triumph.”
Women no longer find sleep because of this fear about their retirement savings
Even if participants in this survey clearly knew how they wanted to be cared for, few people talked about it with their family members.
Almost half of the people who participated in the national study said they were talking with their spouse about the cost of long-term care.
Only 10 percent said they talked to their children about it.
“People want a member of their family to take care of them, but they don’t do what’s necessary to have this conversation,” said Holly Snyder, vice-president of Nationwide’s life insurance branch.
Here's where to start.
Talk to your spouses and children: You can’t prepare your family for care if you don’t give them your wishes well in advance.
Collaborate with your advisor and family to determine where and how you can receive care, as these options can be an important factor in determining the cost.
Call your financial advisor: Your advisor is also able to help you establish a way to pay these fees.
Your long-term care financing decisions may include a traditional long-term care insurance policy, a buy-in-value hybrid life insurance policy to help you cover these expenses, or other insurance on your assets – provided you have the means to insure it.
Prepare your legal documents: Avoid legal battles by the way.
Anticipate a health care proxy that will allow you to designate a trusted person who will check your medical care and ensure that professionals respect your wishes if you are unable to communicate.
Also consider a power of attorney about your financial resources.
You would choose a trusted person who will make financial decisions for you and ensure that your bills are paid if you are disabled.
Suppose your elderly parent has a medical emergency and go to the hospital.
Would you be able to answer questions about medications and allergies?
List these details in a written document to be well-prepared.
“It’s not just the finances that are at stake, but also who are the doctors?” asked Martin.
“Which drugs?
Who will take care of the dog?
Put this plan in place.”
A man was wounded several times with an air rifle in Ilfracombe
A man received several bullets fired with an air rifle as he walked home from an evening.
The forty-year-old victim was in the Oxford Grove area of Ilfracombe, Devon, when he was shot in the chest, stomach and hand.
Officers described the incident, which took place around 2:30 a.m. BST, as a “random act.”
The victim didn’t see his attacker.
His injuries did not threaten his life and the police issued a call for witnesses.
Earthquakes and Tsunamis in Indonesia
At least 384 people have been killed by a powerful earthquake and tsunami that devastated the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, authorities said, and the toll is expected to increase.
The communications being paralyzed, the rescue services could not receive any information from the Donggala regency, an area north of Palu that is further close to the epicenter of the 7.5 magnitude quake.
In Palu, more than 16 000 people were evacuated as a result of the disaster.
Some essential facts about Palu and Donggala, on Sulawesi Island:
Palu is the capital of the central province of Sulawesi, located at the tip of a narrow bay on the west coast of Sulawesi Island, whose population was estimated at 379 800 inhabitants in 2017.
The city celebrated its 40th anniversary when earthquakes and tsunami took place.
Donggala is a regency that runs more than 300 km (180 miles) of coastline northwest of Sulawesi Island.
We estimated the population of the regency, an administrative region located below a province, at 299 200 inhabitants in 2017.
Fisheries and agriculture are the main pillars of the economy of Central Sulawesi Province, and especially in the Coastal Donggala region.
Nickel mining is also an important activity in the province, but it is mainly located in Morowali on the opposite coast of Sulawesi.
Palu and Donggala have been affected by tsunamis several times over the past century, according to the Indonesian Disaster Reduction Agency.
In 1938 a tsunami killed more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of residences in Donggala.
A tsunami also hit western Donggala in 1996, killing nine people.
Indonesia is located on the Pacific Seismic Fire Circle and is regularly shaken by earthquakes.
Here are some of the main earthquakes and tsunamis of recent years:
2004 : A major earthquake on December 26 on the west coast of Indonesia’s Aceh province unleashed a tsunami that surged across 14 countries, killing 226,000 people on the coast of the Indian Ocean, more than half of them in Aceh.
2005 : Several violent earthquakes shaken the west coast of Sumatra from late March to the beginning of April.
Some hundred people have died on the island of Nias off Sumatra.
2006: A 6.8-magnitude earthquake reached southern Java, Indonesia’s most-inhabited island, triggering a tsunami that hit the south coast and claimed nearly 700 lives.
2009: A 7.6-magnitude earthquake shook the city of Padang, capital of West Sumatra province.
More than 1,100 people were killed.
2010: A 7.5-magnitude earthquake devastated one of the Mentawai Islands off Sumatra and triggered a more than 10-metre tsunami that destroyed dozens of villages and killed about 300 people.
2016 : A shallow earthquake shook the regency of Pidie Jaya in Aceh, causing destruction and panic, reminding residents of the devastation caused by the deadly earthquake and tsunami of 2004.
No tsunami was caused this time, but more than 100 people were killed by the collapse of buildings.
2018: Major earthquakes have shaken the Indonesian tourist island of Lombok, killing more than 500 people, mainly on the northern side of the island.
The quake destroyed thousands of buildings and left thousands of tourists temporarily stranded.
Sarah Palin's eldest son arrested for domestic violence
Track Palin, the eldest son of former Alaska Governor and vice-presidency candidate Sarah Palin, has been arrested for assault.
Palin, a 29-year-old from Wasilla, Alaska, was questioned on suspicion of domestic violence, violating a report of spousal violence and resisting arrest, according to a statement issued Saturday by the Alaska State Troopers.
According to the police report, he took the phone of female acquaintance who was trying to call the police to report the alleged crimes.
Palin is remanded in custody in Mat-Su's pre-trial detention centre and is detained on unsecured US$500 bail, KTUU said.
He appeared in court on Saturday, finding himself “not guilty, that’s for sure” when his plea was asked, the network said.
Palin faces three Class A offenses, which means he could be sentenced to up to one year in prison and is punishable by a fine of $250 000.
He was also accused of having committed a minor Class B offence, sanctioned with a day in prison and a fine of US$2 000.
This is not the first time that criminal charges have been laid against Palin.
In December 2017, he was accused of assaulting his father, Todd Palin.
His mother, Sarah Palin, contacted the police to report this alleged assault.
The case is ongoing before the Alaska Veterans Court.
In January 2016, he was charged with spousal violence, interference in the report of a crime of domestic violence and possession of an inebriated weapon in connection with the incident.
His wife had claimed that he had hit her in the face.
Sarah Palin was criticized by veterans’ groups in 2016 after making the connection between her son’s violent behavior and the PTSD stemming from her service in Iraq.
Earthquake in Indonesia : several hundred deaths
At least 384 people died as a result of the earthquake that shaken Indonesia’s Sulawesi island last Friday.
The 7.5-magnitude quake caused a tsunami and ravaged thousands of homes.
The electricity and telecommunications networks are down and the toll of the victims is expected to increase in the coming days.
The quake broke out just off the center of Sulawesi, northeast of Indonesia’s capital Jakarta.
Videos posted on social media show the point of impact.
Several hundred people had gathered for a beach festival in the city of Palu at a time when the tsunami unleashed on the coast.
Federal prosecutors call for rare death penalty for person suspected of terrorist attack in New York
Federal prosecutors in New York are seeking the death penalty for Sayfullo Saipov, New York’s presuming attacker who killed eight victims – a rare sentence that hasn’t been enforced in that state for a federal crime since 1953.
Saipov, 30, is said to have used a truck rented from Home Depot to attack a bike path on West Side Highway in Lower Manhattan, mowing down his pedestrian route and a cyclist last October.
In order to justify a death sentence, prosecutors will have to provide evidence that Saipov “intentionally” murdered the eight victims and “intentionally” inflicted serious bodily harm, according to the statement of intent to apply for the death sentence filed with the Southern District of New York.
Both charges are likely to lead to the death penalty, according to the court document.
A few weeks after this assault, a federal grand jury convicted Saipov of 22 counts, including eighty indictments for extortion of funds, usually used by federal prosecutors in organized delinquency cases and one for crimes of violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack required “meticulous preparation and premeditation,” prosecutors said, calling Saipov’s method “odious, cruel and depraved.”
“Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov has caused injury, damage and loss to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darren Drake, Ariel Erlij, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco,” the statement said.
Five of the victims were Argentinian tourists.
The Southern District of New York had not called for the death penalty in the course of prosecution for ten years.
The defendant, Khalid Barnes, was convicted of the killing of two drug traffickers but was subsequently sentenced to life in prison in September 2009.
The last time the death penalty was applied in a New York federal case was in 1953 in the case concerning Julius and Ethel Rosenberg, a married couple executed for engaging in acts of conspiracy and espionage for the USSR in the two years following the Cold War.
Both Rosenbergs were executed by electric chair on June 19, 1953.
Saipov, originally from Uzbekistan, demonstrated a lack of remorse in the days and months following the tragedy, according to court documents.
According to the police, he told investigators that he was pleased with his actions.
Saipov told the authorities that he had been incited to carry out the attack after watching videos of Daesh on his phone, according to the indictment.
He also asked to display Daesh’s flag in his hospital room, police said.
He pleaded not guilty to 22 counts.
David Patton, one of Saipov’s federal public defenders, said he was “obviously disappointed” by the decision to prosecute.
“We believe that the decision to apply for the death penalty rather than accept his guilty plea and sentence him to life in prison without the possibility of release can only prolong the trauma related to these events for all concerned,” Patton said.
Saipov’s defense team had already asked prosecutors not to claim the death penalty.
