Elite League : Dundee Stars 5-3 Belfast Giants
Patrick Dwyer scored two goals for the Giants against Dundee
The Dundee Stars cleared Friday’s Elite League loss to the Belfast Giants by winning the 5-3 return game in Dundee last Saturday.
The Giants opened the scoring thanks to two goals from Patrick Dwyer and Francis Beauvillier.
Mike Sullivan and Jordan Cownie brought the tie back home before Dwyer returned the advantage to the Giants.
François Bouchard equalized for Dundee before Lukas Lundvald Nielsen scored two goals, assuring victory.
It was the third defeat of the season in the Elite Leagues for Adam Keefe’s players, who beat Dundee 2-1 Friday night in Belfast, after being caught.
It was the season’s fourth showdown between the two sides, with the Giants triumphing in the last three games.
Dwyer gave the lead in the fourth minute, at 3:35, thanks to a pass from Kendall McFaull, while David Rutherford served Beauvillier who doubled the bet four minutes later.
In a busy first period, Sullivan revived the local team at 13:10 before Matt Marquardt put himself at Cownie’s service, at 15:16, with an equalization.
Dwyer secured the Giants’ lead before the first break when he hit his second goal of the night towards the end of the first period.
The local team came together and Bouchard once again equalized with a goal giving a numerical advantage at 27:37.
Cownie and Charles Corcoran collaborated to help Nielsen place Dundee in the lead for the first time of the game at the end of the second period, securing victory for his team by scoring a fifth goal in the mid-course.
The Giants, who have now lost four of their last five games, will host Milton Keynes in their next game on Friday.
An air traffic controller has died helping hundreds of people who were aboard an airplane escaping the earthquake
An Indonesian air traffic controller is hailed as a hero after he has died ensuring that a plane carrying hundreds of people can take off safely.
More than 800 people have died and many others are missing after a major earthquake hit Sulawesi Island last Friday, prompting a tsunami.
Strong aftershocks continue to ravage the area and a large number of people are trapped under rubble in the city of Palu.
But despite fleeing his colleagues, 21-year-old Anthonius Gunawan Agung refused to give up his position in the violently shaken tower at Palu’s Mutiara Sis Al Jufri airport.
He remained there to ensure that the Batik Air Flight 6321, which was on the track at that time, could take off safely.
He then jumped off the control tower when he felt it was collapsing.
He later died in hospital.
Yohannes Sirait, the spokesman for Air Navigation Indonesia, said the action may have saved hundreds of lives, according to the Australian ABC News.
We mobilized a Balikpapan helicopter in Kalimantan to transport it to a larger hospital in another city.
Unfortunately, we lost it this morning before the helicopter arrived in Palu.
“Our heart is broken to learn that,” he added.
The authorities fear that the number of deaths can reach thousands, with the country’s disaster prevention agency indicating that access to the cities of Donggala, Sigi and Boutong is limited.
“The toll would continue to increase because many bodies were still under the rubble and many could not be rescued,” said Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, spokesman for the agency
Waves measuring up to six meters have ravaged Palu which will celebrate a mass burial on Sunday.
Military and commercial aircraft provide aid and food.
Risa Kusuma, a 35-year-old mother, told Sky News: "Every minute an ambulance brings back bodies.
Clean water is rare.
Mini-marketies are looted everywhere.”
Jan Gelfand, head of the International Red Cross in Indonesia, told CNN: “The Indonesian Red Cross runs to help the survivors, but we don’t know what they’re going to find.
It’s already a tragedy, but it could get worse.”
Joko Widodo, Indonesia’s president, arrived in Palu on Sunday and addressed the country’s military: “I ask all of you to work day and night to complete all tasks related to the evacuation.
Are you ready?” said CNN.
Indonesia was rocked earlier this year by earthquakes in Lombok that killed more than 550 people.
