North Korea: the number of deserters “falls” under the direction of Kim
The number of North Korean deserters to South Korea has declined since Kim Jong-un’s rise to power seven years ago, a South Korean lawmaker said.
Park Byeong-seug, citing data from the Ministry of Southern Unification, said 1 127 desertions had taken place last year, up from 2,706 in 2011.
Park said that tightening border controls between North Korea and China and increasing tariffs demanded by smugglers were key factors.
Pyongyang made no public comments.
The vast majority of deserters in the North will ultimately be granted South Korean citizenship.
Seoul claims that more than 30,000 North Koreans have crossed the border illegally since the end of the Korean War in 1953.
Most flee through China, which shares the longest border with North Korea and is easier to cross than the highly-protected demilitarized zone (DMZ), located between the two Koreas.
China sees deserters as illegal migrants rather than refugees and repatriates them often by forcibly.
Relations between the North and the South – which are technically still at war – have improved markedly in recent months.
Earlier this month, the leaders of the two countries gathered in Pyongyang as part of talks focused on denuclearization negotiations that had previously remained outstanding.
This happened after the landmark June meeting between U.S. President Donald Trump and Kim Jong-un in Singapore, during which they broadly agreed to work for a denuclearized Korean peninsula.
However, on Saturday, North Korea’s Foreign Minister Ri Yong-ho blamed U.S. sanctions for the lack of progress ever since.
“In the absence of confidence in the United States, there will be no confidence in our national security and, in such circumstances, it is excluded that we are the first to effect unilateral disarmament,” Ri said in a speech to the United Nations General Assembly in New York.
