How the US-China trade war could benefit Beijing
The first rounds of the U.S.-China trade war have been deafening, and although the battle is far from over, a disagreement between countries could benefit Beijing over the long term, experts said.
U.S. President Donald Trump issued the first warning earlier this year by taxing key Chinese exports, including solar panels, steel and aluminum.
This week’s largest rise with new tariffs affecting US$200 billion (US$150 billion pounds) items, taxing half of all goods from China arriving in the United States.
Each time, Beijing has retaliated in kind, most recently hitting customs duties of 5 to 10% on US$60 billion in U.S. goods.
China has pledged to match the United States, and it is unlikely that the world’s second-largest economy will wobble anytime soon.
To roll back Washington is to give in to the demands, but to yield publicly to the United States would be too embarrassing for Xi Jinping, China’s president.
However, experts believe that if Beijing can play its role properly, the pressures of the U.S. trade war could have a positive effect on China in the long term by reducing the interdependence of both economies.
“The fact that a swift political decision in Washington or Beijing can create conditions conducive to an economic slowdown in either country is actually much more dangerous than what observers have previously recognized,” Abigail Grace, a research associate who focuses on Asia, told the Center for New American Security think tank.
Syria “prepared” for the return of refugees, Foreign Minister said
Syria declares itself ready for the voluntary return of refugees and seeks help to rebuild this country devastated by a war that has lasted for more than seven years.
Addressing the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Walid al-Moualem affirmed that the situation in the country is improving.
“Today, the situation on the ground is more stable and safer thanks to the progress made in the fight against terrorism,” he said.
The government continues to rehabilitate areas destroyed by terrorists in order to restore normalcy.
All the conditions are now in place for the voluntary repatriation of refugees to the country that they have been forced to leave as a result of terrorism and unilateral economic measures aimed at their daily lives and livelihoods.
The United Nations estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have deserted the country since the start of the war in 2011.
Six other million people still living in the country are in need of humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem said that the Syrian regime would welcome aid for the reconstruction of this devastated country.
He stressed, however, that he would not accept conditional aid or aid from countries sponsoring the insurgency.
Europe won the Ryder Cup in Paris
The European team won the 2018 Ryder Cup by beating the United States team with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at the Golf National near Paris, France.
The United States has now lost six times in a row on European soil and have not won a Ryder Cup in Europe since 1993.
Europe regained the title when Danish captain Thomas Bjorn crossed the 14.5 point mark needed to beat the United States.
The American star Phil Mickelson, who found himself in difficulty during most of the tournament, shocked his starting shot in the water at the 16th hole in par-3, ceding his duel to Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari has excelled in all his rounds, becoming one of the four players to have never reached 5-0-0 since the current format of the tournament, whose first edition dates back to 1979.
The American Jordan Spieth has been dismissed 5 to 4 by the less well-placed player of the European team, Thorbjorn Olesen of Denmark.
The world’s best golfer, Dustin Johnson, lost 2 and 1 to England’s Ian Poulter, who may have played his last Ryder Cup.
Veteran of eight Ryder Cups, Spaniard Sergio Garcia has become the all-weathered European champion with a total of 25.5 career points.
“I don’t usually cry, but now it’s stronger than me.
It was an eventful year.
I’m so grateful to Thomas for choosing me and believing in myself.
I'm so happy, so happy to get the cup back.
“What matters is the team, and I’m happy to have been able to help them,” Garcia said with emotion after the European victory.
He passes the torch to his compatriot John Ram who defeated American golf legend Tiger Woods 2-1 in singles last Sunday.
“I feel an incredible pride, beating Tiger Woods, I grew up looking at this guy,” said Rahm, 23.
Woods has lost his four games in France and now holds a career Ryder Cup 13-21-3 record.
A startling statistic for one of the greatest golfers of all time, who won 14 major titles, just behind Jack Nicklaus.
The U.S. team struggled throughout the weekend to find the fairways, with the exception of Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who played high-profile golf for the duration of the tournament.
U.S. captain Jim Furyk commented after a disappointing performance about his team: “I’m proud of these guys, they’ve struggled.
This morning we had a certain moment when we put pressure on Europe.
We've abandoned.
Hat for Thomas.
He's a great captain.
His 12 players all played very well.
We’re going to come together, I’m going to work with the PGA of America and our Ryder Cup committee and we’ll move forward.
“I love these 12 guys and I’m proud to be captain.
We need to bow.
We've been overwhelmed."
Red tide point: Decreased concentrations in Pinellas, Manatee and Sarasota
The latest report from the Florida Fisheries and Wildlife Commission indicates a general decrease in concentrations of the red tide in certain areas of the Tampa Bay area.
According to the CPF, more irregular flowering conditions are reported in areas of Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Charlotte and Collier counties - raising fears of declining concentrations.
The red tide covers approximately 209 km (130 miles) of coastline, from the north of Pinellas south of Lee Counties.
Islets are about 16 km (10 miles) off Hillsborough County, but in fewer sites than last week.
The red tide has also been observed in Pasco County.
Moderated concentrations in or off Pinellas County were reported last week, low to high concentrations off Hillsborough County, higher concentrations in Manatee County, higher concentrations at the bottom of Sarasota County or offshore, average concentrations in Charlotte County, and low concentrations in Lee County and off it, high concentrations at the bottom or offshore in the County of Collier.
Respiratory irritation cases persist in Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier counties.
Respiratory irritation has not been reported in north-west Florida over the past week.
